tel 1.0

base aviation
import soundness

info   dates, times of day, instants, timespans, timezones, calendars and recurrences, checked as the code compiles
detail
    _Aviation_ represents time in five distinct kinds of value, each a type of its own so that
    the mistakes which come from confusing them cannot be written: a **date** on a calendar
    (`Date`, a day-precise `Timestamp`), a **time of day** on a clock (`Clockface`), an
    **instant** on a physical timeline (`Instant over Unix`, `Instant over Tai`, `Instant over
    Monotonic`), the two kinds of **elapsed time** — a `Duration` of SI seconds and a calendar
    `Timespan` of years, months, weeks, days, hours and minutes — and a **moment**, a
    wall-clock reading in a named `Timezone` (`Moment`), which is the one bridge between civil
    and physical time. Wherever a value is known when the code is written it is checked then:
    `2012-Feb-30` does not compile, nor does `13.00.am`, `tz"Mars/Olympus"` or
    `ts"2024-13-01"`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import calendars.gregorianCalendar
    import chronometries.unixChronometry

    val date: Date = 2024-Jan-15
    val appointment: Timestamp = 5.25.pm on date
    val epoch: Instant over Unix = Instant(0L)
    val london: Moment = epoch.in(tz"Europe/London")
    val span = 2*Year + 3*Month + 14*Day
    val later: Date = date + 3*Week
    ```

    A date is written as `year-Mon-day`: `2024 - Jan` is a `Monthstamp` (through
    `Month.monthOfYear`), and `Monthstamp - 15` is the `Date`, validated at compile time by the
    `monthstampSubtraction` operator against the `RomanCalendar` in scope — the Gregorian
    calendar when none is. A date built from values computed at run time goes through the
    calendar's `jdn` (or `Date(year, month, day)`) under a `Tactic[Moment.Error]`, and the
    `ts"…"` interpolator checks an ISO 8601 or RFC 1123 literal, giving a `Year`, `Monthstamp`,
    `Date`, `Timestamp` or `Moment` according to its precision.

    Every date, in every calendar, is the same thing underneath: a Julian day number. A
    `Calendar` is only a way of labelling it with a year, a month and a day, so the
    `calendars` package offers the Gregorian, Julian, Coptic, Ethiopian, Islamic, Persian,
    Indian national, Hebrew and French Republican calendars, the Buddhist and Minguo year
    offsets, the ordinal (day-of-year) calendar and the Papal and British Julian-to-Gregorian
    cutovers, all over one `Date`. Each calendar's month type is a distinct `MonthRadix`, so
    `3*IslamicMonth` can only be added to a date read in the Islamic calendar.

    Rules of civil time which have no single right answer are **policies**, chosen by
    importing a given by name rather than defaulted silently: what `2024-Jan-31 + 1*Month`
    means (`monthEnds`, with no default at all), what the 29th of February is in a common year
    (`nonexistentLeapDays`), what to do with a wall-clock reading inside a spring-forward gap
    (`gapPolicies`, defaulting to `GapPolicy.pushForward`), whether sub-day arithmetic counts
    leap seconds (`leapModes`, defaulting to `LeapMode.lenient`), and which timeline a bare
    `Instant(…)` counts on (`chronometries`). Which day begins the week and which days are the
    weekend is a `Hebdomad` (`hebdomads`), and holidays are a `Holidays` value, which together
    make `date + WorkingDays(5)` meaningful.

    Rendering is composed the same way. A `Date` shows through `dateFormats` (or its
    independent parts, `dateEndianness`, `dateNumerics`, `dateSeparators` and `yearFormats`), a
    `Clockface` through `timeFormats` (or `hourFormats`, `timeMeridiems`, `timeNumerics`,
    `timeSeparators` and `timeSpecificities`), a `Month` through `monthFormats`, a `Weekday`
    through `weekdays`, a `Timespan` relatively ("in 18 minutes") through `timespanFormats`
    and a `Duration` compactly (`1h 0m 5s`) through `durationFormats`; `instantDecodables` and
    `durationDecodables` read instants and durations back from text. None of these is in scope
    after `import soundness.*` alone, since a wildcard import does not import givens: each is
    imported by name, as `import dateFormats.iso8601DateFormat`.

    The current time is read through a `Clock` (`now()`, `today()`), whose companion default
    reads the system wall clock, and elapsed time through a `MonotonicClock` (`monotonic()`); a
    test binds `given Clock = Clock.fixed(Instant(0L))` and time stands still. Recurring
    events are `Recurrence`s (ISO 8601 repeating intervals, `rec"R5/2024-01-01/P1D"`) and
    `Rrule`s (RFC 5545 calendar rules), both `Recurrent`, so `occurrences`, `until`, `within`
    and `following` serve either.

    The library has two components:

    - `aviation.core` — everything above, for the JVM, Scala.js and Scala Native. The
      `Clock.current` and `MonotonicClock.current` givens read `System.currentTimeMillis` and
      `System.nanoTime`; timezone rules come from `java.time` (and `Tzdb` parses the IANA
      database's own source files for rules newer than the platform's).
    - `aviation.wasi` — `clocks.wasiClock` and `clocks.wasiMonotonicClock`, which read the
      `wasi:clocks` interfaces of a WebAssembly component through `wasiApis.wasiClockApi`,
      for code compiled to a Wasm target.

    Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella, and every choice package here merges
    with the umbrella's own, so `import soundness.*` followed by
    `import calendars.gregorianCalendar` is the usual opening.

refer aviation.Date
refer aviation.Timestamp
refer aviation.Clockface
refer aviation.Instant
refer aviation.Moment
refer aviation.Timespan
refer aviation.Calendar
refer aviation.calendars
refer aviation.Clock

entry .Date   a day on the calendar: a `Timestamp in Day`, held as a Julian day number
    `Date` is the type alias `Timestamp in Day` — a `Timestamp` whose `Form` is day precision,
    with the time of day clamped to midnight — so a date is used wherever a timestamp is, and
    every `Timestamp` extension (`jdn`, `year`, `month`, `day`, `weekday`, `yearDay`,
    `anniversary`, `monthstamp`, `in`, `addDays` and the comparisons) applies to it. The
    object `Date` is declared in the hidden `timestampInternal`, which lists its members.

    A literal date is written `2024-Jan-15` and checked while the code compiles: the month
    must have the day, in the `RomanCalendar` in scope or the Gregorian calendar when there is
    none, so `2012-Feb-30` and `2011-Feb-29` are compile errors and `2012-Feb-29` is not. The
    operands must be literals (or, for the calendar, one of `calendars.gregorianCalendar`,
    `julianCalendar`, `papalCutover` or `britishCutover`, possibly through a local alias): a
    year or day computed at run time halts the expansion with a message naming it, and such a
    date is built with `Date(year, month, day)` instead, which raises `Moment.Error` under a
    `Tactic[Moment.Error]` when the date does not exist in the `Calendar` in scope.
    `Date.julianDay(n)` makes a date directly from its Julian day number, with no check.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import calendars.gregorianCalendar

    val literal: Date = 2024-Jan-15
    val computed: Date = Date(Year(2024), Month(2), Day(29))
    val parsed: Date = t"2011-12-13".as[Date]
    val typed: Date = ts"2024-12-31"

    (literal.year(), literal.month, literal.day())   // (2024, Jan, 15)
    literal.weekday                                  // Mon
    literal.yearDay                                  // 15
    literal.jdn                                      // 2460325
    literal < computed                               // true
    literal.addDays(31)                              // 2024-Feb-15
    computed - literal                               // 45 days, as a Quanta[Days[1]]
    ```

    A date moves by a `Timespan` (`date + 3*Day`, `date + 2*Week`, needing nothing; `date +
    1*Month` or `date + 1*Year`, needing a `Disambiguation` from `monthEnds` because the
    result may not exist), by `WorkingDays` (needing a `Hebdomad` and a `Holidays`), and by
    whole days as a `Quanta[Days[1]]`; two dates subtract to a `Quanta[Days[1]]`. The
    `Timestamp` companion holds these givens.

    Text: `t"2011-12-13".as[Date]` decodes a `year-month-day` of digits (one or two digits for
    the month and day), raising `Moment.Error` for anything else; `date.encode` is the ISO
    `2024-01-15`, needing a `RomanCalendar`; `date.show` needs an `Endianness`, a
    `Date.Numerics`, a `Date.Separation` and a `Years`, or one of the `dateFormats` givens
    which bundles the four; and a date inspects as its ISO Gregorian form,
    `2024-01-15T00:00:00`, with no context at all.

entry .Timestamp   a date and a time of day, with no timezone, at a precision in its type
    `Timestamp` is the opaque type `timestampInternal.Timestamp`, exported here: a zoneless
    point on a millisecond grid, packed into one `Long` as the Julian day number times the
    milliseconds in a day plus the millisecond of the day. Its precision is the phantom type
    member `Form`, set with `in`: `Date` is `Timestamp in Day` and `Monthstamp` is
    `Timestamp in Month`, so a date or a month is a timestamp and every timestamp extension
    applies to it. A `Timestamp` carries no timezone and means no instant; grounding it to an
    `Instant` goes through a `Moment` (`timestamp.in(timezone).instant`, or `timestamp.instant`
    with a `Timezone`, a `RomanCalendar` and a `GapPolicy` in scope).

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import calendars.gregorianCalendar

    val built: Timestamp = Timestamp(2023-May-28, Clockface(14, 30, 59))
    val joined: Timestamp = 5.25.pm on 2018-Aug-11
    val parsed: Timestamp = t"2024-01-15T14:30:59".as[Timestamp]
    val literal: Timestamp = ts"2023-05-28T14:30:59"

    (built.date, built.time)              // (2023-May-28, Clockface(14, 30, 59))
    (built.hour, built.minute, built.second)   // (14, 30, 59)
    built.in(tz"Europe/London")           // a Moment
    built.stdlib                          // a java.time.LocalDateTime
    ```

    Two timestamps subtract to a `Timespan` of days, hours, minutes and seconds
    (`Timestamp.timestampSubtractable`, the `Difference` type), and compare with `<`, `<=`,
    `>` and `>=` or through `Timestamp.timestampOrderable`. `t"…".as[Timestamp]` reads
    `yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss` (a space or `T` between), raising `Timestamp.Error`, and `show`
    renders "time, date" from the `Clockface is Showable` and `Date is Showable` in scope.
    A timestamp inspects as its ISO Gregorian form, `2023-05-28T14:30:59`, with milliseconds
    only when they are not zero.

    The companion's givens — the decoder, the showables, the date and month arithmetic, the
    working-day arithmetic, the `Orderable` and `Comparable` instances and `Timestamp.Error` —
    are listed under the hidden `timestampInternal`, as are the extensions.

entry .Instant   a point in physical time, on a timeline named by its type: `Instant over Unix`
    `Instant` is the opaque type `protointernal.Instant`, exported here: a `Long` whose
    meaning — which timeline it counts on, and at what resolution — is the phantom type
    member `Transport`, set with `over`. `Instant over Unix` counts milliseconds of POSIX time
    from 1970, with leap seconds ignored; `Instant over Tai` counts milliseconds of
    International Atomic Time; `Instant over Monotonic` counts nanoseconds of the system's
    monotonic clock, from an arbitrary origin. The unrefined `Instant` is abstract in its
    timeline and rarely used directly.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import chronometries.unixChronometry
    import calendars.gregorianCalendar

    val epoch: Instant over Unix = Instant(0L)
    val later: Instant over Unix = epoch + 1*Hour
    later - epoch                              // 3600 seconds, a Duration
    later.long                                 // 3600000L
    epoch.in(tz"UTC").date                     // 1970-Jan-1
    epoch.over[Tai].long                       // 10000L: ten leap seconds ahead in 1970
    epoch ~ later                              // a Period[Instant over Unix]
    ```

    `Instant(millis)` takes its timeline from the `Chronometry.Ambient` in scope, which is why
    `import chronometries.unixChronometry` opens most programs; `Instant.of[Unix](millis)`
    names the timeline directly. An instant moves by any `Quantity` of time (`+ 1*Hour`,
    `- 30*Second`), and two instants on the same timeline subtract to a `Duration`;
    subtracting across timelines is a compile error, and `over[Tai]` or `over[Unix]`
    converts, through the `Chronometry` of each. `in(timezone)` places an instant in a zone
    as a `Moment`, and `timestamp` does the same with the `Timezone` in scope. `Instant.Min`
    and `Instant.Max` are the ends of a timeline, and an instant inspects as `⧗0`, with an
    hourglass so that it cannot be mistaken for the `Long` it erases to. The companion's
    givens and the extensions are listed under the hidden `protointernal`.

entry .Duration   a physical quantity of elapsed time: `Quantity[Seconds[1]]`
    `Duration` is the type alias `protointernal.Duration`, a quantitative `Quantity[Seconds[1]]`,
    exported here. It is the elapsed time between two instants, the same everywhere and
    always, and is deliberately not a `Timespan`, which counts calendar units whose length
    depends on where they are anchored. `Duration(millis)` makes one from milliseconds, and
    `duration.value` is its seconds; `duration.from(instant)` is the `Period` which starts at
    the instant and lasts the duration.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import chronometries.unixChronometry

    Duration(1000L).value                     // 1.0
    (Instant(3600000L) - Instant(0L)).value   // 3600.0
    Duration(90000L).from(Instant(0L))        // Period(Instant(0L), Instant(90000L))
    ```

    `durationDecodables.compactDurationDecodable` reads `30m`, `1.5h`, `250ms` or a bare `90`
    from text, raising `Duration.Error` (error 335, _`text` is not a recognized duration_) for
    anything else, and `durationFormats.compactDurationFormat` shows a duration as `1h 0m 5s`.
    The alias, the companion and its `Error` are under the hidden `protointernal`.

entry .Year   a calendar year, as an opaque `Int`
    `Year` is the opaque type `internal.Year`, exported here. `Year(2024)` makes one and
    `year()` reads the number back. Years add and subtract whole counts (`Year(2024) + 5`),
    order (`Year(1999) < Year(2000)`), decode from text (`t"2024".as[Year]`), show as the
    bare number and inspect as `2024ʸ`, with a suffix so that the number is not mistaken for
    an `Int`. `Year` is also the irregular `Radix` of a `Timespan`: `2*Year` is a timespan of
    two years, and `date + 1*Year` needs a `Disambiguation` from `monthEnds`, since the
    result of adding a year to the 29th of February is a policy. The companion's givens are
    listed under the hidden `internal`.

entry .Day   a day of the month, as an opaque `Int`
    `Day` is the opaque type `internal.Day`, exported here. `Day(15)` makes one and `day()`
    reads the number back; it decodes from text, shows as the number and inspects as `15ᵈ`.
    `Day` is also the regular `Radix` of a `Timespan`: `3*Day` is a timespan of three days,
    which adds to any date with no policy, since a day is always a day. It is distinct from
    `WorkingDays`, a count of working days. The companion's givens are listed under the
    hidden `internal`.

entry .Anniversary   a month and a day with no year: a birthday, a holiday
    `Anniversary` is the opaque type `internal.Anniversary`, exported here, written `Mar - 14`
    (through `Month.subtractable`) or `Anniversary(Mar, Day(14))`. Applying it to a year gives
    that year's date, `(Mar - 14)(Year(2024))`, which needs a `RomanCalendar` and an
    `Anniversary.NonexistentLeapDay` policy in scope for the one anniversary which does not
    exist every year, the 29th of February: `nonexistentLeapDays.roundDownLeapDay` lands on
    the 28th, `roundUpLeapDay` on the 1st of March, and `raiseErrorsLeapDay` raises
    `Moment.Error`. The policy has no default.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import calendars.gregorianCalendar
    import nonexistentLeapDays.roundDownLeapDay

    val leapDay: Anniversary = Feb - 29
    leapDay(Year(2023))   // 2023-Feb-28
    leapDay(Year(2024))   // 2024-Feb-29
    (leapDay.month, leapDay.day)   // (Feb, 29)
    ```

    An anniversary shows with the `Endianness`, `Months` and `Date.Separation` in scope
    (`14 March` or `March 14`), and inspects as ISO 8601's yearless `--03-14`. `date.anniversary`
    is the anniversary of any date. The companion is listed under the hidden `internal`.

entry .WorkingDays   a count of working days, to add to or subtract from a date
    `WorkingDays` is the opaque type `internal.WorkingDays`, exported here. `WorkingDays(5)`
    is five working days, and `date + WorkingDays(5)` or `date - WorkingDays(1)` moves a date
    over that many days which are neither weekend days — as the `Hebdomad` in scope defines
    them — nor holidays in the `Holidays` in scope, landing on a working day; both givens are
    required. It inspects as `5ʷᵈ`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import calendars.gregorianCalendar
    import hebdomads.europeanHebdomad

    given Holidays = Holidays(List(Holiday(2025-Dec-25, t"Christmas Day"),
        Holiday(2025-Dec-26, t"Boxing Day")))

    2025-Aug-11 + WorkingDays(5)   // 2025-Aug-18, over the weekend
    2025-Dec-24 + WorkingDays(1)   // 2025-Dec-29, over the holidays and the weekend
    WorkingDays(5)()               // 5
    ```

entry .Base60   the integers 0 to 59, as a union of literal types: a minute or a second
  detail
      `Base60` is the type `0 | 1 | … | 59`, the type of a `Clockface`'s `minute` and
      `second`, so that `Clockface(14, 30, 59)` typechecks from literals and `Clockface(14,
      60, 0)` does not. `Base60(int)` wraps an integer modulo 60 (so `Base60(61)` is `1` and
      `Base60(-1)` is `59`), and `Base60.unapply` matches an `Int` in range, which is how a
      parsed field becomes one: `(hour, minute, second).absolve match case (Base24(h),
      Base60(m), Base60(s)) => Clockface(h, m, s)`. The `base60Extractable` given extracts
      one from text in the same way.
  entry .apply   an integer reduced modulo 60 to a `Base60`
  entry .unapply   matches an `Int` between 0 and 59, as a `Base60`

entry .Base24   the integers 0 to 23, as a union of literal types: an hour
  detail
      `Base24` is the type `0 | 1 | … | 23`, the type of a `Clockface`'s `hour`. `Base24(int)`
      wraps an integer modulo 24 (so `Base24(25)` is `1`), and `Base24.unapply` matches an
      `Int` in range; `base24Extractable` extracts one from text.
  entry .apply   an integer reduced modulo 24 to a `Base24`
  entry .unapply   matches an `Int` between 0 and 23, as a `Base24`

entry .base60Extractable   extracts a `Base60` from text which reads as an integer in range
    A top-level given: with an `Int` extractor from text in scope, `text match case As[Base60](m)
    => …` matches text holding an integer from 0 to 59, and `Unset` otherwise. It is imported
    by name, `import soundness.base60Extractable`, as any top-level given is.

entry .base24Extractable   extracts a `Base24` from text which reads as an integer in range
    A top-level given, the counterpart of `base60Extractable` for hours: `As[Base24](h)`
    matches text holding an integer from 0 to 23.

entry .MonthRadix   the radix of some calendar's months, distinct for each calendar
    A `MonthRadix` is an irregular `Radix` — one whose ratio to the unit below it is not
    constant — standing for the months of one calendar: `Month` (Gregorian), `IslamicMonth`,
    `CopticMonth`, `EthiopianMonth`, `HebrewMonth`, `IndianMonth`, `PersianMonth` and
    `FrenchRepublicanMonth` each extend it. A `Timespan of IslamicMonth.type` is a span counted
    in Islamic months, which adds only to a date read under the `IslamicCalendar`: adding it
    under the Gregorian calendar is a compile error, so months of different calendars never
    mix.

entry .Week   the radix of weeks: `2*Week` is a `Timespan of Week.type`
  detail
      A regular `Radix` (seven days, always), so `date + 2*Week` needs no policy. `Int is
      Multiplicable by Week.type` is the given which makes `n*Week` a timespan.
  entry .multiplicable   `n*Week` as a `Timespan of Week.type`

entry .Hour   the radix of hours, and the precision marker for `Timestamp in Hour`
  detail
      `Hour` is both a regular `Radix` object, so that `2*Hour` is a `Timespan of Hour.type`
      which adds to an `Instant`, a `Timestamp` or a `Moment`, and an erased marker trait, so
      that `Timestamp in Hour` names an hour-precise timestamp. The `Multiplicable` given is
      in its companion.
  entry .multiplicable   `n*Hour` as a `Timespan of Hour.type`

entry .Minute   the radix of minutes, and the precision marker for `Timestamp in Minute`
  detail
      As `Hour`: a regular `Radix` object (`15*Minute`) and an erased marker trait.
  entry .multiplicable   `n*Minute` as a `Timespan of Minute.type`

entry .Second   the precision marker for `Timestamp in Second`
    An erased marker trait with no members, so that a second-precise timestamp has a type to
    name. The radix of seconds in a timespan is quantitative's `Second` unit, so `30*Second` is
    a `Quantity` of seconds, which adds to an instant or a moment as a `Duration`.

entry .Tai   the marker of International Atomic Time: `Instant over Tai`
    An erased marker trait naming the TAI timeline, which counts every SI second with no
    discontinuity. `Chronometry.tai` is its (identity) chronometry and `Resolution.tai` its
    millisecond resolution; `chronometries.taiChronometry` makes it the timeline of bare
    `Instant(…)` values. `instant.over[Tai]` converts a Unix instant to it by adding the leap
    seconds which had accumulated by that instant.

entry .Unix   the marker of POSIX time: `Instant over Unix`
    An erased marker trait naming the Unix timeline — milliseconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z
    with leap seconds ignored, which is what every system wall clock reads. `Chronometry.unix`
    converts it to and from TAI through `LeapSeconds`, `Resolution.unix` is its millisecond
    resolution, a `Clock` produces instants on it, and `chronometries.unixChronometry` makes
    it the timeline of bare `Instant(…)` values.

entry .Monotonic   the marker of the monotonic system clock: `Instant over Monotonic`
    An erased marker trait naming the timeline of `System.nanoTime` readings: nanosecond
    resolution (`Resolution.monotonic`), an arbitrary origin, and no `Chronometry`, since it
    has no fixed relation to wall-clock time. A `Monotonic` instant can be compared with and
    subtracted from another, and moved by a `Duration`, but never placed in a timezone or
    converted to another timeline; `monotonic()` reads one.

entry .Occurrence   which of a repeated wall-clock time a `Moment` means, in a fall-back overlap
    When the clocks go back, an hour of wall-clock time happens twice, and a `Moment` in it
    names two instants an hour apart. `First` is the earlier, under the offset before the
    transition; `Second` is the later, after it. A `Moment` is built with `Occurrence.First`
    unless another is given, grounding ignores the occurrence for a time which happens once,
    and `instant.in(timezone)` records `Second` for an instant which falls on the later side
    of an overlap, so that the moment grounds back to the instant it came from.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import calendars.gregorianCalendar

    val clock = Clockface(1, 30, 0)
    val earlier = Moment(2024-Oct-27, clock, tz"Europe/London")
    val later = Moment(2024-Oct-27, clock, tz"Europe/London", Occurrence.Second)
    later.instant.long - earlier.instant.long   // 3600000
    ```

entry .Leap   whether a `Moment` is an inserted leap second, the 61st second of a UTC day
    A `Clockface` second is a `Base60`, so the leap second `23:59:60` is stored as `23:59:59`
    with the moment's `leap` set to `Inserted`; `None` is every other moment. Only a `Moment`
    grounds or shows it as the 61st second: `ts"2016-12-31T23:59:60Z"` is such a moment, and
    `moment.tai` lands on the TAI second the leap second occupies.

entry .am   a time of day from a literal such as `7.25.am`, checked as the code compiles
    An inline extension on a `Double` literal: `7.25.am` is `Clockface(7, 25, 0)`, `12.00.am`
    is midnight and `0.00.am` the same. The literal must have exactly two digits after the
    point (the minutes), an hour of at most 12 and minutes below 60: `7.8.am`, `7.585.am`,
    `7.88.am` and `13.00.am` are compile errors, each with its own message. The time is
    always an exact minute; `Clockface(h, m, s)` is for seconds.

entry .pm   a time of day from a literal such as `7.25.pm`: the afternoon
    As `am`, twelve hours later: `7.25.pm` is `Clockface(19, 25, 0)` and `12.00.pm` is noon.
    `15.30.pm` is a compile error, since the hour of a twelve-hour clock is at most 12.

entry .now   the current instant, read from the `Clock` in scope
    `now()` is `Instant over Unix`, the reading of the `Clock` given in scope. `Clock.current`,
    in the companion, reads `System.currentTimeMillis` and needs no import; a test binds
    `given Clock = Clock.fixed(Instant(99999L))` so that `now()` is the same every time.

    ```scala fuel
    frame
      label The system clock, through `Clock.current`
      code
          locally:
            import chronometries.unixChronometry
            now()           // whatever the wall clock reads, as an Instant over Unix
            now() == now()  // false, once a millisecond has passed
    frame
      label A fixed clock, bound as a given
      code
          locally:
            import chronometries.unixChronometry
            given Clock = Clock.fixed(Instant(99999L))
            now()           // Instant(99999L)
            now() == now()  // true
    ##
    ```

entry .monotonic   a reading of the monotonic clock, for measuring elapsed time
    `monotonic()` is `Instant over Monotonic`, the reading of the `MonotonicClock` given in
    scope: `MonotonicClock.current`, in the companion, reads `System.nanoTime`, and
    `clocks.wasiMonotonicClock` reads a Wasm component's monotonic clock. The reading has
    nanosecond resolution and an arbitrary origin, so it is only ever compared with or
    subtracted from another, giving a `Duration`; it cannot be placed in a timezone.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val start = monotonic()
    (start + 1*Second).long - start.long   // 1000000000L
    ((start + 90*Second) - start).value    // 90.0
    ```

entry .today   the current date, in the `Timezone` and `RomanCalendar` in scope
    `today()` reads `now()` from the `Clock` in scope, places it in the `Timezone` in scope
    and takes the date, read in the `RomanCalendar` in scope: all three are required.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import calendars.gregorianCalendar
    import chronometries.unixChronometry

    given Clock = Clock.fixed(Instant(0L))
    given Timezone = tz"UTC"
    today()   // 1970-Jan-1
    ```

entry .tz   the `tz"…"` interpolator: a `Timezone`, checked against the database as the code compiles
    `tz"Europe/London"` is a `Timezone` whose name the compiler has confirmed is a zone the
    `java.time` database knows, so a misspelled zone is a compile error, _`Europe/Lundon` is
    not a valid timezone identifier_. The interpolator takes no `$`-substitutions; a name only
    known at run time is `Timezone(name)` or `text.as[Timezone]`, raising `Timezone.Error`.

entry .dur   the `dur"…"` interpolator: an ISO 8601 duration as a `Timespan`, checked as the code compiles
    `dur"P1Y2M3DT4H5M6S"` is the `Timespan` of one year, two months, three days, four hours,
    five minutes and six seconds, parsed while the code compiles: `P` then any of `nY`, `nM`,
    `nW`, `nD`, and after `T` any of `nH`, `nM`, `nS` (the seconds may have a fraction), with
    at least one component present. `M` before the `T` is months and after it minutes. A text
    which is not a duration is a compile error, and `t"P1Y".as[Timespan]` is the same parse at
    run time, raising `Timespan.Error`.

entry .ts   the `ts"…"` interpolator: a year, month, date, timestamp or moment literal, by its precision
    `ts"…"` parses an ISO 8601 or RFC 1123 literal while the code compiles, and its result
    type follows the literal's precision, through the transparent `tsInterpolator`:

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val year: Year = ts"2024"
    val month: Monthstamp = ts"2024-12"
    val date: Date = ts"2024-12-31"
    val timestamp: Timestamp = ts"2023-05-28T14:30:59"
    val moment: Moment = ts"2023-05-28T14:30:59Z"
    val offset: Moment = ts"2023-05-28T14:30:59+02:00"
    val rfc: Moment = ts"Tue, 17 Jun 2024 14:30:45 GMT"
    ```

    The date is validated against the Gregorian calendar (`ts"2024-02-30"` is a compile error,
    _2024-2-30 is not a valid date in the Gregorian calendar_), the time against the clock
    (`25:00` fails), and the zone against `java.time`. Fractional seconds are kept to the
    nanosecond, a space may stand for the `T`, and a leap second `23:59:60` is accepted only
    with a zone (it exists only relative to UTC), giving a `Moment` whose `leap` is
    `Leap.Inserted`. An RFC 1123 literal always gives a `Moment` in GMT. The literal takes no
    `$`-substitutions.

entry .rec   the `rec"…"` interpolator: an ISO 8601 repeating interval as a `Recurrence`, checked as the code compiles
    `rec"R5/2024-01-01/P1D"` is `Recurrence(2024-Jan-1, 1*Day, 5)`: a repetition count (`R`
    alone for unbounded, `Rn` for `n` occurrences), a start, and a period, separated by `/`.
    The start is parsed as `ts"…"` parses it and must be a date or a date-time (a bare year or
    month is a compile error), so the result is a `Recurrence of Date`, `of Timestamp` or `of
    Moment` by the literal's precision; the period is parsed as `dur"…"` parses it. A failing
    segment is underlined on its own. The period is tagged with the Gregorian month radix, so
    iterating a monthly recurrence needs a `Disambiguation` from `monthEnds`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import calendars.gregorianCalendar
    import monthEnds.clampMonthEnd

    rec"R3/2024-01-01/P1M".occurrences   // Chain(2024-Jan-1, 2024-Feb-1, 2024-Mar-1)
    ```

entry .tsInterpolator   the `Interpolable` behind `ts"…"`, with a result type chosen by the literal
    An inline given whose transparent `interpolate` expands the `ts"…"` literal to a `Year`,
    `Monthstamp`, `Date`, `Timestamp` or `Moment`, through the macro `internal.tsInterpolator`.
    It is in implicit scope for the union type, so `ts"…"` needs no import beyond the umbrella.

entry .recInterpolator   the `Interpolable` behind `rec"…"`, with a result type chosen by the literal
    An inline given whose transparent `interpolate` expands the `rec"…"` literal to a
    `RecurrenceLiteral` of the start's precision, through `internal.recInterpolator`.

entry .RecurrenceLiteral   the result type of `rec"…"`: a recurrence of a date, timestamp or moment
    The union `(Recurrence of Date by (Timespan of Month.type)) | (Recurrence of Timestamp by
    …) | (Recurrence of Moment by …)`. The period is a `Timespan of Month.type` so that its
    calendar components add correctly to each kind of start; the transparent interpolator
    narrows the union to the one member the literal has.

entry .occurrences   the occurrences of any `Recurrent` value, as a lazy `Chain`
    An extension on any value with a `Recurrent` instance — a `Recurrence`, an `Rrule`, a
    `RecurrenceSet` — giving its ordered stream of occurrence points. The chain is lazy and
    may be infinite (a recurrence with no count, a rule with no `until`), so it is bounded
    with `until`, `within`, `following` or `keep(n)` before it is traversed.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import calendars.gregorianCalendar

    Recurrence(2024-Jan-1, 1*Day, 3).occurrences   // Chain(2024-Jan-1, 2024-Jan-2, 2024-Jan-3)
    Rrule(2024-Jan-15, Frequency.Monthly, count = 2).occurrences
    ```

entry .until   the occurrences of a `Recurrent` value strictly before a limit
    `sequence.until(limit)` keeps the occurrences which compare less than `limit`, needing a
    `Comparable` for the occurrence type (`Date`, `Timestamp`, `Instant` and `Moment` each have
    one; a `Moment`'s needs a `RomanCalendar` and a `GapPolicy`).

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import calendars.gregorianCalendar

    Recurrence(2024-Jan-1, 1*Week).until(2024-Jan-22)   // Chain(2024-Jan-1, 2024-Jan-8, 2024-Jan-15)
    ```

entry .within   the occurrences of a `Recurrent` value inside a half-open `Period`
    `sequence.within(period)` skips the occurrences before the period's start and keeps those
    before its finish, so the start is included and the finish excluded, as a `Period` is.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import calendars.gregorianCalendar

    Recurrence(2024-Jan-1, 1*Week).within(Period(2024-Jan-5, 2024-Jan-20))
    // Chain(2024-Jan-8, 2024-Jan-15)
    ```

entry .following   the first occurrence of a `Recurrent` value strictly after a point, if any
    `sequence.following(point)` is the first occurrence greater than `point`, or `Unset` when
    the sequence ends first. The occurrences before it are skipped lazily, so an unbounded
    recurrence is fine.

entry .Jan   January, exported from `Month`
    The first case of the `Month` enum, exported to the package so that a date is written
    `2024-Jan-15` without qualifying it. `Jan` to `Dec` are all exported; `Month(1)` and
    `Month(t"Jan")` reach the same case from a number or a name.
entry .Feb   February, exported from `Month`
entry .Mar   March, exported from `Month`
entry .Apr   April, exported from `Month`
entry .May   May, exported from `Month`
entry .Jun   June, exported from `Month`
entry .Jul   July, exported from `Month`
entry .Aug   August, exported from `Month`
entry .Sep   September, exported from `Month`
entry .Oct   October, exported from `Month`
entry .Nov   November, exported from `Month`
entry .Dec   December, exported from `Month`

entry .Mon   Monday, exported from `Weekday`
    The first case of the `Weekday` enum, exported to the package so that `Weekday.count(start,
    end, Sat)` or `WeekdayOrdinal(Mon, 3)` needs no qualifier. `Mon` to `Sun` are all exported.
    Which weekday begins the week, and which are the weekend, is the `Hebdomad` in scope.
entry .Tue   Tuesday, exported from `Weekday`
entry .Wed   Wednesday, exported from `Weekday`
entry .Thu   Thursday, exported from `Weekday`
entry .Fri   Friday, exported from `Weekday`
entry .Sat   Saturday, exported from `Weekday`
entry .Sun   Sunday, exported from `Weekday`

entry .calendars   the calendars, each a given to import by name
  detail
      Every date is a Julian day number, and a calendar is a way of reading it as a year, a
      month and a day, so a date literal, `date.year`, `date.month`, `date.day` and month
      arithmetic each take the `Calendar` (or `RomanCalendar`) given in scope. The choice is
      never implicit: `import soundness.*` brings none of these in, and one is imported by
      name, most often `import calendars.gregorianCalendar`.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label The Gregorian calendar, the civil default
        code
            val date: Date = { import calendars.gregorianCalendar; 2000-Jan-1 }
            locally:
              import calendars.gregorianCalendar
              (date.year(), date.month, date.day())   // (2000, Jan, 1)
      frame
        label The same day in the Julian calendar
        code
            val date: Date = { import calendars.gregorianCalendar; 2000-Jan-1 }
            locally:
              import calendars.julianCalendar
              (date.year(), date.month, date.day())   // (1999, Dec, 19)
      frame
        label The same day in the Coptic calendar
        code
            val date: Date = { import calendars.gregorianCalendar; 2000-Jan-1 }
            locally:
              import calendars.copticCalendar
              (date.year(), date.month, date.day())   // (1716, CopticMonth.Koiak, 22)
      frame
        label The same day in the Islamic calendar
        code
            val date: Date = { import calendars.gregorianCalendar; 2000-Jan-1 }
            locally:
              import calendars.islamicCalendar
              (date.year(), date.month, date.day())   // (1420, IslamicMonth.Ramadan, 24)
      ##
      ```

      A calendar is also a value: `calendars.islamicCalendar.format(date)` renders a date in
      its own month names (`24 Ramadan 1420`), and `calendar.jdn(year, month, day)` builds a
      date from its parts, raising `Moment.Error` for one the calendar does not have. The two
      Roman calendars and the cutover regimes are `RomanCalendar`s, which is what `today()`,
      `Moment` and a date literal require; the others are plain `Calendar`s, read with
      `calendar.annual(date)`, `mensual` and `diurnal` or through `date.year` and the rest
      when bound as the `Calendar` in scope.
  refer aviation.Calendar
  refer aviation.RomanCalendar
  refer aviation.Regime
  entry .gregorianCalendar   the Gregorian calendar, proleptic: the civil default
      A `RomanCalendar` named `Gregorian`, whose leap years are those divisible by 4 but not by
      100, unless by 400. It is proleptic, applying the rule before 1582 as well; a date
      before the reform in the calendar actually in use is `papalCutover`'s or
      `britishCutover`'s business. Date literals and `ts"…"` are validated against it when no
      other `RomanCalendar` is in scope.
  entry .julianCalendar   the Julian calendar, with a leap year every fourth year
      A `RomanCalendar` named `Julian`: every year divisible by 4 is a leap year, so it drifts
      from the Gregorian calendar by three days every four centuries, and `1900-Mar-10` under
      it is a different Julian day number from the same literal under the Gregorian calendar.
  entry .copticCalendar   the Coptic calendar, from 29 August 284 Julian
      A `CopticCalendar`: thirteen `CopticMonth`s, twelve of thirty days and the short `Nasie`.
  entry .ethiopianCalendar   the Ethiopian calendar, from 29 August 8 Julian
      An `EthiopianCalendar`: the Coptic structure with its own epoch and `EthiopianMonth`s.
  entry .islamicCalendar   the tabular Islamic (Hijri) calendar, from 16 July 622 Julian
      An `IslamicCalendar`: twelve `IslamicMonth`s alternating 30 and 29 days, with eleven leap
      years in each cycle of thirty. It is the arithmetical calendar, not the observed one.
  entry .persianCalendar   the Persian (Solar Hijri) calendar
      A `PersianCalendar`, with `PersianMonth`s.
  entry .indianCalendar   the Indian national (Saka) calendar, from 22 March 1957
      An `IndianCalendar`: a Saka year is the Gregorian year less 78, beginning on 22 March (21
      March in a Gregorian leap year), with `IndianMonth`s.
  entry .hebrewCalendar   the Hebrew calendar, lunisolar, with thirteen months in a leap year
      A `HebrewCalendar`: `HebrewMonth`s from Tishrei, with `AdarSheni` present only in the
      seven leap years of each nineteen, and years of 353 to 385 days.
  entry .frenchRepublicanCalendar   the French Republican calendar of 1793 to 1805
      A `FrenchRepublicanCalendar`: twelve thirty-day `FrenchRepublicanMonth`s and the
      `Sansculottides`, from 22 September 1792, with the arithmetical leap rule.
  entry .buddhistCalendar   the Gregorian calendar with years numbered from 543 BCE
      An `OffsetCalendar` over `gregorianCalendar` named `Buddhist`, adding 543 to the year, as
      Thailand numbers them.
  entry .minguoCalendar   the Gregorian calendar with years numbered from 1912
      An `OffsetCalendar` over `gregorianCalendar` named `Minguo`, subtracting 1911 from the
      year, as Taiwan numbers them.
  entry .ordinalCalendar   the year and day-of-year calendar, with no months
      The `OrdinalCalendar` object: a date is a year and a day within it, 1 to 366, built with
      `OrdinalCalendar(year, dayOfYear)`.
  entry .papalCutover   the Julian calendar until 4 October 1582, the Gregorian from 15 October
      A `Regime` named `Papal` of two segments: Julian from the beginning of time, Gregorian
      from Julian day 2299161. The ten days between were never dates, so `1582-Oct-10` under
      it is a compile error, `papalCutover.jdn(Year(1582), Oct, Day(10))` raises
      `Moment.Error`, and `(1582-Oct-4).jdn + 1 == (1582-Oct-15).jdn`.
  entry .britishCutover   the Julian calendar until 2 September 1752, the Gregorian from 14 September
      A `Regime` named `British`: Julian until Julian day 2361222, Gregorian from it, as
      Britain and its colonies adopted the reform, with eleven days dropped.

entry .nonexistentLeapDays   what the 29th of February is in a common year, a given to import by name
  detail
      Applying an `Anniversary` to a `Year`, `(Feb - 29)(Year(2023))`, needs an
      `Anniversary.NonexistentLeapDay` in scope for the one anniversary a common year lacks.
      There is no default; one of these three is imported by name, and it is consulted only
      when the date does not exist, so a leap year is unaffected by the choice.
  entry .roundUpLeapDay   the 29th of February becomes the 1st of March
  entry .roundDownLeapDay   the 29th of February becomes the 28th
  entry .raiseErrorsLeapDay   the 29th of February raises `Moment.Error` in a common year
      Needs a `Tactic[Moment.Error]` where it is imported, which the given captures and
      raises `Moment.Error(_.Invalid(year, 2, 29, gregorianCalendar))` through.

entry .monthEnds   what adding months or years to a date means when the day overflows, a given to import by name
  detail
      `2024-Jan-31 + 1*Month` asks for the 31st of February, and `2024-Feb-29 + 1*Year` for
      the 29th of February 2025, neither of which exists. The `Disambiguation` in scope
      decides, and there is deliberately no default, so month or year arithmetic on a date is
      a compile error until one of these is imported by name. Day and week arithmetic needs
      none, since those never overflow.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label Clamping to the last day of the month
        code
            locally:
              import calendars.gregorianCalendar
              import monthEnds.clampMonthEnd
              2024-Jan-31 + 1*Month   // 2024-Feb-29
              2024-Feb-29 + 1*Year    // 2025-Feb-28
      frame
        label Overflowing into the next month
        code
            locally:
              import calendars.gregorianCalendar
              import monthEnds.overflowMonthEnd
              2024-Jan-31 + 1*Month   // 2024-Mar-2
              2024-Feb-29 + 1*Year    // 2025-Mar-1
      ##
      ```
  refer aviation.Disambiguation
  entry .clampMonthEnd   a day past the end of the month becomes its last day
  entry .overflowMonthEnd   a day past the end of the month spills into the next by the excess
  entry .raiseMonthEnd   a day past the end of the month raises `Moment.Error`
      Needs a `Tactic[Moment.Error]` where it is imported, through which the given raises
      `Moment.Error(_.Invalid(year, month, day, calendar))`.

entry .gapPolicies   what a wall-clock time inside a spring-forward gap means, a given to import by name
  detail
      When the clocks go forward, an hour of wall-clock time never happens, and a `Moment` in
      it — `Moment(2024-Mar-31, Clockface(1, 30, 0), tz"Europe/London")` — must be grounded
      somehow. The `GapPolicy` in scope decides; `GapPolicy.pushForward`, the companion's
      default, keeps the old offset and so lands just after the gap, as `java.time` does, and
      these two override it.
  refer aviation.GapPolicy
  entry .pushBackwardGapPolicy   a time in the gap is grounded just before it
      Takes the `backward` candidate: the instant under the offset after the transition,
      which falls just before the gap opens.
  entry .rejectGapPolicy   a time in the gap raises `Moment.Error`
      Needs a `Tactic[Moment.Error]` where it is imported, through which the given raises
      `Moment.Error(_.Gap)`, _the wall-clock time does not exist in its timezone_.

entry .leapModes   whether sub-day arithmetic on a `Moment` counts leap seconds, a given to import by name
  detail
      `moment + 2*Hour` under the default `LeapMode.lenient` adds two hours of wall-clock time
      on the leap-free POSIX line, so a leap second between is invisible. Importing
      `exactLeapMode` adds two hours of real SI seconds, through the TAI timeline, so that
      crossing an inserted leap second lands the result one wall-clock second earlier. The
      two agree except across one of the historical leap instants.
  refer aviation.LeapMode
  entry .exactLeapMode   sub-day arithmetic counts real SI seconds, through TAI
      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import calendars.gregorianCalendar
      import leapModes.exactLeapMode

      val start = Moment(2016-Dec-31, Clockface(23, 0, 0), tz"UTC")
      (start + 2*Hour).instant   // 2017-01-01T00:59:59Z: a second behind the lenient answer
      ```

entry .chronometries   the timeline a bare `Instant(…)` counts on, a given to import by name
  detail
      `Instant(millis)`, and every decoder which makes an instant from a `Long`, takes its
      timeline from the `Chronometry.Ambient` in scope, which names the `Transport` of the
      result. There is no default: a program imports one, almost always `unixChronometry`,
      since that is what every wall clock reads; `instant.over[Tai]` converts afterwards.
  refer aviation.Chronometry
  entry .unixChronometry   bare instants are `Instant over Unix`: POSIX milliseconds
  entry .taiChronometry   bare instants are `Instant over Tai`: atomic-time milliseconds

entry .hebdomads   which day begins the week and which days are the weekend, a given to import by name
  detail
      A `Hebdomad` is consulted by `date.weekend`, `weekday.weekend`, `weekday.number`,
      `Weekday(ordinal)` and the working-day arithmetic `date + WorkingDays(n)`. There is no
      default; one of these is imported by name, or a `Hebdomad` is defined locally.
  refer aviation.Hebdomad
  entry .europeanHebdomad   the week starts on Monday; Saturday and Sunday are the weekend
  entry .northAmericanHebdomad   the week starts on Sunday; Saturday and Sunday are the weekend
  entry .jewishHebdomad   the week starts on Sunday; Friday and Saturday are the weekend

entry .instantDecodables   how an `Instant over Unix` is read from text, a given to import by name
  detail
      `t"…".as[Instant over Unix]` needs a `Decodable` for the instant, and two standard
      formats are offered. Each needs a `Tactic[Moment.Error]`, which it raises for text which
      does not conform, with the format, the offset of the fault and an `Issue` saying what
      was expected there.
  refer aviation.Iso8601
  refer aviation.Rfc1123
  entry .iso8601InstantDecodable   reads ISO 8601 date-times, dates, months and week dates, with `Z` or an offset
      `Iso8601.parse`: `1994-11-06T08:49:37Z`, `2023-05-28T14:30:59.123+02:00`, the basic
      form `20230528T143059Z`, `2023-W21-7T14:30:59Z`, a date `2020-12-31` or a month
      `2012-11` (midnight UTC), hours alone or hours and minutes, a fraction on the last
      field, and a leap second `23:59:60`, which lands on the following second.
  entry .rfc1123InstantDecodable   reads RFC 1123 date-times, `Tue, 17 Jun 2024 14:30:45 GMT`
      `Rfc1123.parse`: the form HTTP headers use, with a two-digit day and `GMT`.

entry .dateFormats   how a `Date` shows, as a whole convention, a given to import by name
  detail
      `date.show` needs a `Date is Showable`, and each given here is one, bundling an
      `Endianness`, a `Date.Numerics`, a `Date.Separation` and a `Years` into a convention.
      For a convention not listed, import one given from each of `dateEndianness`,
      `dateNumerics`, `dateSeparators` and `yearFormats` instead, and `Timestamp.dateShowable`
      assembles the format from them. The year, month and day are read in the Gregorian
      calendar whatever calendar is in scope.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label ISO 8601
        code
            val date: Date = { import calendars.gregorianCalendar; 2025-Apr-7 }
            locally:
              import dateFormats.iso8601DateFormat
              date.show   // 2025-04-07
      frame
        label The European convention
        code
            val date: Date = { import calendars.gregorianCalendar; 2025-Apr-7 }
            locally:
              import dateFormats.europeanDateFormat
              date.show   // 07.04.2025
      frame
        label The American convention
        code
            val date: Date = { import calendars.gregorianCalendar; 2025-Apr-7 }
            locally:
              import dateFormats.americanDateFormat
              date.show   // 04/07/2025
      frame
        label The British convention
        code
            val date: Date = { import calendars.gregorianCalendar; 2025-Apr-7 }
            locally:
              import dateFormats.unitedKingdomDateFormat
              date.show   // 07/04/2025
      frame
        label Composed from its parts
        code
            val date: Date = { import calendars.gregorianCalendar; 2025-Apr-7 }
            locally:
              import dateEndianness.bigEndian
              import dateNumerics.variableWidthDateNumerics
              import dateSeparators.hyphenDateSeparator
              import yearFormats.twoDigitsYears
              date.show   // 25-4-7
      ##
      ```
  entry .europeanDateFormat   day, month, year, two digits each, with dots: `07.04.2025`
  entry .americanDateFormat   month, day, year, with slashes: `04/07/2025`
  entry .unitedKingdomDateFormat   day, month, year, with slashes: `07/04/2025`
  entry .southEastAsiaDateFormat   day, month, year, with hyphens: `07-04-2025`
  entry .iso8601DateFormat   year, month, day, with hyphens: `2025-04-07`

entry .dateEndianness   the order of a shown date's fields, a given to import by name
  detail
      An `Endianness`, one of the four parts `Timestamp.dateShowable`, `Anniversary.showable`
      and `Timestamp.monthShowable` compose a rendering from. Imported by name, or bundled by
      a `dateFormats` given.
  entry .bigEndian   year, month, day
  entry .littleEndian   day, month, year
  entry .middleEndian   month, day, year

entry .dateNumerics   whether a shown date's month and day are padded to two digits, a given to import by name
  detail
      A `Date.Numerics`: one of the four parts of a composed date rendering. Imported by name,
      or bundled by a `dateFormats` given.
  entry .fixedWidthDateNumerics   the month and day are two digits: `04`, `07`
  entry .variableWidthDateNumerics   the month and day are as many digits as they need: `4`, `7`

entry .dateSeparators   the character between a shown date's fields, a given to import by name
  detail
      A `Date.Separation`: one of the four parts of a composed date rendering, also used
      between an `Anniversary`'s or a `Monthstamp`'s fields. Imported by name, or bundled by a
      `dateFormats` given.
  entry .slashDateSeparator   `/`
  entry .hyphenDateSeparator   `-`
  entry .dotDateSeparator   `.`
  entry .spaceDateSeparator   a space

entry .yearFormats   whether a shown date's year has four digits or two, a given to import by name
  detail
      A `Years`: one of the four parts of a composed date rendering, also used by
      `Timestamp.monthShowable`. Imported by name, or bundled by a `dateFormats` given.
  entry .fullYears   the whole year: `2025`
  entry .twoDigitsYears   the year modulo 100, padded: `25`

entry .weekdays   the names of the weekdays, in a language and a length, a given to import by name
  detail
      A `Weekdays` maps each `Weekday` to its name, and is what `weekday.show` and the
      localised description of an `Rrule` need. One is imported by name;
      `weekdays.englishWeekdays.name(Mon)` reads a name directly.
  entry .englishWeekdays   `Monday` to `Sunday`
  entry .englishShortWeekdays   `Mon` to `Sun`
  entry .oneLetterAmbiguousWeekdays   `M`, `T`, `W`, `T`, `F`, `S`, `S`: a calendar's column heads
  entry .shortestUnambiguousWeekdays   `M`, `Tu`, `W`, `Th`, `F`, `Sa`, `Su`
  entry .twoLetterWeekdays   `Mo` to `Su`
  entry .frenchWeekdays   `lundi` to `dimanche`
  entry .germanWeekdays   `Montag` to `Sonntag`
  entry .spanishWeekdays   `lunes` to `domingo`

entry .monthFormats   the names of the months, in a language and a length, a given to import by name
  detail
      A `Months` maps each `Month` to its name, and is what `month.show`, `Anniversary.showable`,
      `Timestamp.monthShowable` and the localised description of an `Rrule` need. One is
      imported by name; `monthFormats.englishMonths.name(Jan)` reads a name directly.
  entry .englishMonths   `January` to `December`
  entry .englishShortMonths   `Jan` to `Dec`
  entry .numericMonths   the month's number, `1` to `12`
  entry .twoDigitMonths   the month's number padded to two digits, `01` to `12`
  entry .oneLetterAmbiguousMonths   `J`, `F`, `M`, `A`, `M`, `J`, `J`, `A`, `S`, `O`, `N`, `D`
  entry .frenchMonths   `janvier` to `décembre`
  entry .germanMonths   `Januar` to `Dezember`
  entry .spanishMonths   `enero` to `diciembre`

entry .timeFormats   how a `Clockface` shows, as a whole convention, a given to import by name
  detail
      `time.show` needs a `Clockface is Showable`, and each given here is one, bundling a
      `Clockface.Format` (from `hourFormats`), a `Clockface.Numerics`, a `Clockface.Separation`
      and a `Clockface.Specificity`, with a `Meridiem is Showable` for the twelve-hour forms.
      For a convention not listed, import one given from each of those packages instead, and
      `Clockface.showable` assembles the format.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label Twenty-four hours, no separator
        code
            val time = Clockface(14, 30, 59)
            locally:
              import timeFormats.militaryTimeFormat
              time.show   // 1430
      frame
        label Twelve hours and a meridiem
        code
            val time = Clockface(14, 30, 59)
            locally:
              import timeFormats.civilianTimeFormat
              time.show   // 02:30 PM
      frame
        label The Associated Press style
        code
            val time = Clockface(14, 30, 59)
            locally:
              import timeFormats.associatedPressTimeFormat
              time.show   // 2:30 p.m.
      frame
        label ISO 8601, with seconds
        code
            val time = Clockface(14, 30, 59)
            locally:
              import timeFormats.iso8601TimeFormat
              time.show   // 14:30:59
      ##
      ```
  entry .militaryTimeFormat   `1430`: twenty-four hours, two digits, no separator
  entry .civilianTimeFormat   `02:30 PM`: twelve hours, two digits, a colon and an upper-case meridiem
  entry .associatedPressTimeFormat   `2:30 p.m.`: twelve hours, no padding, a colon and a punctuated meridiem
  entry .frenchTimeFormat   `14h30`: twenty-four hours with an `h` between
  entry .iso8601TimeFormat   `14:30:59`: twenty-four hours, colons, and seconds
  entry .ledgerTimeFormat   `14.30`: twenty-four hours with a dot between
  entry .railwayTimeFormat   `14:30`: twenty-four hours with a colon between

entry .hourFormats   whether a shown time has twelve hours or twenty-four, and seconds, a given to import by name
  detail
      A `Clockface.Format`: whether the hour is on a twelve-hour clock, whether seconds are
      shown, and what follows the time (the meridiem, for a twelve-hour clock, which needs a
      `Meridiem is Showable` from `timeMeridiems` in scope). One of the four parts of a
      composed time rendering; imported by name, or bundled by a `timeFormats` given.
  entry .twelveHourClock   hours 1 to 12, a space and the meridiem, no seconds
  entry .twelveHourSecondsClock   hours 1 to 12 and the meridiem; declared to show seconds
      Note that as declared it shows no seconds either: its `seconds` is `false`, the same as
      `twelveHourClock`.
  entry .twentyFourHourClock   hours 0 to 23, no meridiem, no seconds
  entry .twentyFourHourSecondsClock   hours 0 to 23, no meridiem, with seconds

entry .timeMeridiems   how `AM` and `PM` are written, a given to import by name
  detail
      A `Meridiem is Showable`, which the twelve-hour `hourFormats` need. `Meridiem.showable`,
      in the companion, is `AM`/`PM` and needs no import; these are the alternatives.
  entry .upperMeridiem   `AM`, `PM`
  entry .lowerMeridiem   `am`, `pm`
  entry .upperPunctuatedMeridiem   `A.M.`, `P.M.`
  entry .lowerPunctuatedMeridiem   `a.m.`, `p.m.`

entry .timeSpecificities   whether a shown time goes to minutes or seconds, a given to import by name
  detail
      A `Clockface.Specificity`, required by `Clockface.showable` as one of its four parts.
      Whether seconds are actually written is decided by the `Clockface.Format`'s `seconds`.
  entry .minutesSpecificity   to the minute
  entry .secondsSpecificity   to the second

entry .timeNumerics   whether a shown time's hour is padded to two digits, a given to import by name
  detail
      A `Clockface.Numerics`: one of the four parts of a composed time rendering. The minutes
      and seconds are always two digits; only the hour varies.
  entry .fixedWidthTimeNumerics   the hour is two digits: `02:30`
  entry .variableWidthTimeNumerics   the hour is as many digits as it needs: `2:30`

entry .timeSeparators   the character between a shown time's hours and minutes, a given to import by name
  detail
      A `Clockface.Separation`, used between the hours and minutes and, unless overridden,
      between the minutes and seconds. One of the four parts of a composed time rendering.
  entry .colonTimeSeparator   `:`
  entry .dotTimeSeparator   `.`
  entry .noneTimeSeparator   nothing: `1430`
  entry .frenchTimeSeparator   `h`: `14h30`

entry .durationDecodables   how a `Duration` is read from text, a given to import by name
  detail
      `t"…".as[Duration]` needs a `Decodable`, and the one offered reads the compact form a
      command line uses.
  entry .compactDurationDecodable   reads `30m`, `1.5h`, `250ms`, `2d` or a bare count of seconds
      A decimal count followed by `ms`, `s`, `m`, `h` or `d`, or seconds when there is no
      unit, surrounding whitespace ignored: `t"1.5h".as[Duration].value` is `5400.0` and
      `t"90".as[Duration]` ninety seconds. A negative, a word or an empty text raises
      `Duration.Error` through the `Tactic[Duration.Error]` the given needs.

entry .durationFormats   how a `Duration` shows, a given to import by name
  detail
      `duration.show` needs a `Duration is Showable`; without one a `Duration`, being a
      `Quantity`, shows as quantitative renders it (`1.5 s`).
  entry .compactDurationFormat   `5s`, `2m 5s`, `1h 0m 5s`, `2d 1h 0m 5s`, or `420ms` below a second
      The coarsest unit present first and every unit after it down to seconds, so that a column
      of durations lines up; below a second, milliseconds alone. A negative duration takes a
      leading `-`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import durationFormats.compactDurationFormat

      Duration(3605000L).show   // 1h 0m 5s
      Duration(420L).show       // 420ms
      ```

entry .timespanFormats   how a `Timespan` shows relative to now, in a language, a given to import by name
  detail
      `span.show` without one of these is the ISO 8601 duration (`PT18M`); with one, it is a
      relative phrase — `in 18 minutes`, `8 minutes ago`, `12 hours and 38 minutes ago`, `just
      now` — with only the non-zero components, coarsest first, and the future or past form
      by the sign. Each given needs a `Locale` of its language in scope (`given Locale[en] =
      Locale(en)`), so several may be imported at once and the locale selects between them.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import timespanFormats.englishRelativeTimespan

      given Locale[en] = Locale(en)
      Timespan(minutes = 18).show   // in 18 minutes
      Timespan(minutes = -8).show   // 8 minutes ago
      Timespan().show               // just now
      ```
  refer aviation.Vernacular
  entry .englishRelativeTimespan   `in 18 minutes`, `8 minutes ago`, `just now`; needs a `Locale[en]`
  entry .frenchRelativeTimespan   `dans 18 minutes`, `il y a 8 minutes`; needs a `Locale[fr]`
  entry .germanRelativeTimespan   `in 18 Minuten`, `vor 8 Minuten`; needs a `Locale[de]`
  entry .spanishRelativeTimespan   `en 18 minutos`, `hace 8 minutos`; needs a `Locale[es]`

entry .Calendar   how a Julian day number is read as a year, a month and a day
  detail
      A `Calendar` labels dates. Every `Date` is a Julian day number, the same in every
      calendar, and a calendar supplies the arithmetic between that number and a year
      (`#Annual`, always `Year`), a month (`#Mensual`, the calendar's own month type) and a
      day of the month (`#Diurnal`, always `Day`): `#annual`, `#mensual` and `#diurnal` read a
      date's parts, `#computeJdn` builds a date from parts it trusts, and `#jdn` builds one
      from parts it checks, raising `Moment.Error` for a day the month does not have. `Date`'s
      extensions `year`, `month` and `day` take the `Calendar` in scope and delegate to it.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import calendars.islamicCalendar

      val calendar: Calendar = islamicCalendar
      val ramadan: Date = calendar.jdn(Year(1445), IslamicMonth.Ramadan, Day(15))
      calendar.format(ramadan)                              // 15 Ramadan 1445
      calendar.daysInMonth(IslamicMonth.Ramadan, Year(1445))   // 30
      calendar.monthsInYear(Year(1445))                     // 12
      calendar.leapYear                                     // does not compile
      ```

      The `#MonthUnit` type member is the calendar's `MonthRadix`, which ties a `Timespan of
      IslamicMonth.type` to the `IslamicCalendar` so that month arithmetic dispatches to the
      right calendar and a span of one calendar's months cannot be added under another. The
      standard calendars are givens in the `calendars` package, imported by name; a calendar of
      one's own extends `Calendar` (or `Enumerated`, when its months are an enum, or
      `AlexandrianCalendar` or `RomanCalendar` for the two common structures), supplying a
      `Showable` for its month type. Months are numbered by `#monthOrdinal` and
      `#monthOfOrdinal` from zero, year by year, since a lunisolar calendar's numbering shifts
      in a leap year.
  refer aviation.calendars
  refer aviation.RomanCalendar
  refer aviation.Enumerated
  refer aviation.AlexandrianCalendar
  entry #Annual   the calendar's year type: `Year`
  entry #Diurnal   the calendar's day-of-month type: `Day`
  entry #Mensual   the calendar's month type, which must be `Showable`
      `Month` for the Roman calendars, `IslamicMonth`, `CopticMonth`, `HebrewMonth` and so on
      for the others. An enum month is `Showable` through spectacular's enumeration instance,
      rendering its case name, unless its companion refines it as `HebrewMonth` and
      `IslamicMonth` do.
  entry #MonthUnit   the calendar's `MonthRadix`, the radix of a timespan of its months
  entry #name   the calendar's name, as `Moment.Error` reports it: `Gregorian`, `Islamic`
  entry #monthsInYear   how many months a year has: twelve, thirteen for an Alexandrian or leap Hebrew year
  entry #daysInYear   how many days a year has
  entry #daysInMonth   how many days a month has in a year
  entry #monthOrdinal   a month's position in a year, from zero
      Year-dependent for the Hebrew calendar, where `Nisan` is the seventh month in a leap
      year and the sixth in a common one; constant for every other calendar.
  entry #monthOfOrdinal   the month at a position in a year, from zero: the inverse of `#monthOrdinal`
  entry #annual   the year a date falls in
  entry #mensual   the month a date falls in
  entry #diurnal   the day of the month a date falls on
  entry #zerothDayOfYear   the day before a year's first day, so that `date.jdn - zerothDayOfYear(year).jdn` is the day of the year
  entry #computeJdn   the date of a year, month and day which are known to be valid
      The calendar's arithmetic alone, with no range check: `computeJdn(Year(2024), Feb,
      Day(31))` is the 2nd of March. `#jdn` is the checked form.
  entry #validDate   whether a year, month and day name a real date
      The default checks the day against `#daysInMonth`; a `Regime` overrides it to reject the
      dates in its cutover gap.
  entry #jdn   the date of a year, month and day, checked, raising `Moment.Error` otherwise
      Inline, so that the `Tactic[Moment.Error]` it needs is resolved where it is called.
      Raises `Moment.Error(_.Invalid(year, month, day, calendar))` (error 333, _`2024-2-30`
      does not exist in the calendar Gregorian_) and returns `#computeJdn`'s date.
  entry #monthName   a month's display name: its `Showable` rendering
  entry #format   a date as `day month-name year` in this calendar: `15 Ramadan 1445`

entry .Enumerated   a calendar whose months are the cases of one enum, numbered the same in every year
  detail
      `Enumerated[month](fromOrdinal)` fixes `Mensual` to the enum type and defines
      `monthOrdinal` as the case's ordinal and `monthOfOrdinal` through the enum's
      `fromOrdinal`, leaving the rest of `Calendar` to the subclass. `RomanCalendar`,
      `IslamicCalendar`, `IndianCalendar`, `PersianCalendar` and the Alexandrian calendars
      extend it; `HebrewCalendar` does not, since its numbering shifts in a leap year.
  entry #monthOrdinal   the enum case's ordinal, whatever the year
  entry #monthOfOrdinal   the enum case at an ordinal, whatever the year

entry .AlexandrianCalendar   the shared shape of the Coptic, Ethiopian and French Republican calendars
  detail
      Twelve months of thirty days, a short thirteenth month of five days (six in a leap year),
      and a leap year every fourth year — those whose number modulo 4 is 3. A calendar of this
      shape supplies only `#epoch`, the Julian day number of its first day, and its name and
      month type: `CopticCalendar`, `EthiopianCalendar` and `FrenchRepublicanCalendar` are
      the three, differing only in epoch and month names.
  refer aviation.CopticCalendar
  refer aviation.EthiopianCalendar
  refer aviation.FrenchRepublicanCalendar
  entry #epoch   the Julian day number of the calendar's year 1, month 1, day 1
  entry #monthsInYear   thirteen
  entry #leapYear   whether a year is a leap year: those whose number modulo 4 is 3
  entry #daysInYear   366 in a leap year, else 365
  entry #daysInMonth   thirty for the first twelve months; five or six for the thirteenth
  entry #zerothDayOfYear   the day before a year's first day, from the epoch
  entry #annual   the year a date falls in, from the epoch
  entry #mensual   the month a date falls in: a thirty-day block, or the thirteenth month after day 360
  entry #diurnal   the day of the month a date falls on
  entry #computeJdn   the date of a year, month and day, by the thirty-day arithmetic

entry .CopticCalendar   the Coptic calendar, from 1 Thout 1 AM, Julian day 1825030
  detail
      An `AlexandrianCalendar` over `CopticMonth`, named `Coptic`, whose epoch is 29 August 284
      CE in the Julian calendar. `calendars.copticCalendar` is the given.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import calendars.copticCalendar

      val date: Date = { import calendars.gregorianCalendar; 2000-Jan-1 }
      (copticCalendar.annual(date)(), copticCalendar.mensual(date), copticCalendar.diurnal(date)())
      // (1716, CopticMonth.Koiak, 22)
      ```
  entry #MonthUnit   `CopticMonth.type`
  entry #epoch   1825030
  entry #name   `Coptic`

entry .CopticMonth   the thirteen months of the Coptic calendar, and the radix of a span of them
  detail
      `Thout`, `Paopi`, `Hathor`, `Koiak`, `Tobi`, `Meshir`, `Paremhat`, `Parmouti`, `Pashons`,
      `Paoni`, `Epip`, `Mesori` and the short `Nasie`. The companion is a `MonthRadix`, so
      `3*CopticMonth` is a `Timespan of CopticMonth.type`, which adds to a date only under the
      `CopticCalendar` (with a `Disambiguation` from `monthEnds` in scope). A month shows as
      its case name.
  entry .multiplicable   `n*CopticMonth` as a `Timespan of CopticMonth.type`

entry .EthiopianCalendar   the Ethiopian calendar, from 1 Mäskäräm 1, Julian day 1724221
  detail
      An `AlexandrianCalendar` over `EthiopianMonth`, named `Ethiopian`, whose epoch is 29
      August 8 CE in the Julian calendar: the Coptic structure, seven to eight years behind the
      Coptic reckoning. `calendars.ethiopianCalendar` is the given.
  entry #MonthUnit   `EthiopianMonth.type`
  entry #epoch   1724221
  entry #name   `Ethiopian`

entry .EthiopianMonth   the thirteen months of the Ethiopian calendar, and the radix of a span of them
  detail
      `Meskerem`, `Tekemt`, `Hedar`, `Tahsas`, `Ter`, `Yekatit`, `Megabit`, `Miyazya`, `Genbot`,
      `Sene`, `Hamle`, `Nehase` and the short `Pagume`. The companion is a `MonthRadix`, so
      `n*EthiopianMonth` is a span of Ethiopian months, which adds to a date only under the
      `EthiopianCalendar`.
  entry .multiply   `n*EthiopianMonth` as a `Timespan of EthiopianMonth.type`

entry .FrenchRepublicanCalendar   the French Republican calendar, from 1 Vendémiaire An I, Julian day 2375840
  detail
      An `AlexandrianCalendar` over `FrenchRepublicanMonth`, named `French Republican`, whose
      epoch is 22 September 1792. Its leap rule is the arithmetical one (years whose number
      modulo 4 is 3), which matches the sextile years III, VII and XI observed during the
      calendar's use from 1793 to 1805. `calendars.frenchRepublicanCalendar` is the given.
  entry #MonthUnit   `FrenchRepublicanMonth.type`
  entry #epoch   2375840
  entry #name   `French Republican`

entry .FrenchRepublicanMonth   the twelve months and the complementary days of the French Republican calendar
  detail
      `Vendemiaire`, `Brumaire`, `Frimaire`, `Nivose`, `Pluviose`, `Ventose`, `Germinal`,
      `Floreal`, `Prairial`, `Messidor`, `Thermidor`, `Fructidor`, each of thirty days, and the
      `Sansculottides`, the five or six complementary days which fill out the year, as a
      thirteenth month. The companion is a `MonthRadix`.
  entry .multiply   `n*FrenchRepublicanMonth` as a `Timespan of FrenchRepublicanMonth.type`

entry .IslamicCalendar   the tabular Islamic (Hijri) calendar, from 1 Muharram 1 AH, Julian day 1948440
  detail
      An `Enumerated` calendar over `IslamicMonth`, named `Islamic`: twelve months alternating
      thirty and twenty-nine days, with a thirtieth day added to the last month in the eleven
      leap years of each thirty-year cycle (the "Kuwaiti" rule: years where `(11*year + 14) %
      30 < 11`). Its civil epoch is 16 July 622 CE in the Julian calendar. This is the
      arithmetical calendar; the observed calendar, which depends on sightings of the moon,
      may differ by a day. `calendars.islamicCalendar` is the given.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import calendars.islamicCalendar

      islamicCalendar.format(Date(Year(1445), IslamicMonth.Ramadan, Day(15)))   // 15 Ramadan 1445
      ```
  entry #MonthUnit   `IslamicMonth.type`
  entry #name   `Islamic`
  entry #monthsInYear   twelve
  entry #leapYear   whether a year has 355 days: eleven in every thirty
  entry #daysInYear   355 in a leap year, else 354
  entry #daysInMonth   thirty for odd-numbered months, twenty-nine for even, thirty for the twelfth in a leap year
  entry #zerothDayOfYear   the day before a year's first day
  entry #annual   the year a date falls in
  entry #mensual   the month a date falls in
  entry #diurnal   the day of the month a date falls on
  entry #computeJdn   the date of a year, month and day, by the tabular arithmetic

entry .IslamicMonth   the twelve months of the Islamic calendar, and the radix of a span of them
  detail
      `Muharram`, `Safar`, `RabiAlAwwal`, `RabiAlThani`, `JumadaAlAwwal`, `JumadaAlThani`,
      `Rajab`, `Shaban`, `Ramadan`, `Shawwal`, `DhuAlQadah` and `DhuAlHijjah`. The companion
      is a `MonthRadix` (`n*IslamicMonth` adds to a date only under the `IslamicCalendar`) and
      supplies the `Showable` which renders the names with their conventional spacing and
      numerals: `Rabi I`, `Jumada II`, `Dhu al-Hijjah`.
  entry .showable   the month's conventional name: `Rabi I`, `Dhu al-Qadah`
  entry .multiply   `n*IslamicMonth` as a `Timespan of IslamicMonth.type`

entry .IndianCalendar   the Indian national (Saka) calendar, tied to the Gregorian year
  detail
      An `Enumerated` calendar over `IndianMonth`, named `Indian National`. A Saka year is the
      Gregorian year less 78, and begins on 1 Chaitra, which is 22 March, or 21 March when the
      Gregorian year is a leap year; Chaitra then has thirty days (thirty-one in a leap year),
      the next five months thirty-one and the last six thirty. India adopted it in 1957, when
      1 Chaitra 1879 fell on 22 March. `calendars.indianCalendar` is the given.
  entry #MonthUnit   `IndianMonth.type`
  entry #name   `Indian National`
  entry #monthsInYear   twelve
  entry #leapYear   whether the Gregorian year the Saka year begins in is a leap year
  entry #daysInYear   366 in a leap year, else 365
  entry #daysInMonth   thirty or thirty-one for Chaitra, thirty-one for the next five months, thirty for the rest
  entry #zerothDayOfYear   the day before 1 Chaitra
  entry #annual   the Saka year a date falls in
  entry #mensual   the month a date falls in
  entry #diurnal   the day of the month a date falls on
  entry #computeJdn   the date of a year, month and day, from 1 Chaitra of the year

entry .IndianMonth   the twelve months of the Indian national calendar, and the radix of a span of them
  detail
      `Chaitra`, `Vaishakha`, `Jyeshtha`, `Ashadha`, `Shravana`, `Bhadra`, `Ashwin`, `Kartika`,
      `Agrahayana`, `Pausha`, `Magha` and `Phalguna`. The companion is a `MonthRadix`.
  entry .multiply   `n*IndianMonth` as a `Timespan of IndianMonth.type`

entry .HebrewCalendar   the Hebrew calendar: lunisolar, with a thirteenth month in seven years of nineteen
  detail
      A `Calendar` over `HebrewMonth`, named `Hebrew`, from 1 Tishrei AM 1 at Julian day
      347998. The year begins at Rosh Hashanah, fixed by the mean lunar conjunction (the
      *molad*) and adjusted by the four postponement rules, so a year runs to 353, 354 or 355
      days — 383, 384 or 385 in a leap year — with Cheshvan and Kislev absorbing the
      variation. Leap years, which insert Adar I before Adar II, follow the nineteen-year
      Metonic cycle. It is not `Enumerated`: a month's position in the year depends on
      whether the year is leap, so `#monthOrdinal` and `#monthOfOrdinal` take the year
      seriously. `calendars.hebrewCalendar` is the given.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import calendars.hebrewCalendar

      hebrewCalendar.format(Date(Year(5784), HebrewMonth.AdarSheni, Day(1)))   // 1 Adar II 5784
      hebrewCalendar.leapYear(Year(5784))                                     // true
      hebrewCalendar.monthsInYear(Year(5785))                                 // 12
      ```
  entry #Mensual   `HebrewMonth`
  entry #MonthUnit   `HebrewMonth.type`
  entry #name   `Hebrew`
  entry #leapYear   whether a year has thirteen months: seven in every nineteen
  entry #monthsInYear   thirteen in a leap year, else twelve
  entry #monthOrdinal   a month's position in the year, with `Nisan` onwards one lower in a common year
  entry #monthOfOrdinal   the month at a position, skipping `AdarSheni` in a common year
  entry #daysInYear   the days from one Rosh Hashanah to the next: 353 to 385
  entry #daysInMonth   thirty or twenty-nine; `Cheshvan` and `Kislev` vary with the year's length, `Adar` with leap years
  entry #zerothDayOfYear   the day before Rosh Hashanah
  entry #annual   the year a date falls in
  entry #mensual   the month a date falls in
  entry #diurnal   the day of the month a date falls on
  entry #computeJdn   the date of a year, month and day, counted from Rosh Hashanah

entry .HebrewMonth   the thirteen months of the Hebrew calendar, and the radix of a span of them
  detail
      In civil-year order from `Tishrei`: `Tishrei`, `Cheshvan`, `Kislev`, `Tevet`, `Shevat`,
      `Adar`, `AdarSheni`, `Nisan`, `Iyar`, `Sivan`, `Tammuz`, `Av` and `Elul`. In a common year
      `AdarSheni` does not occur and `Adar` is the only Adar; in a leap year `Adar` is Adar I
      and `AdarSheni` is Adar II. The companion is a `MonthRadix`, and its `Showable` renders
      `AdarSheni` as `Adar II`.
  entry .showable   the month's name, with `AdarSheni` as `Adar II`
  entry .multiply   `n*HebrewMonth` as a `Timespan of HebrewMonth.type`

entry .Clock   a source of the current `Instant over Unix`
  detail
      `now()` and `today()` read the `Clock` given in scope. `Clock.current`, in the companion,
      reads `System.currentTimeMillis` and so is found with no import; `Clock.fixed` and
      `Clock.offset` make clocks for tests and for simulating another time, bound as a local
      given, and `clocks.wasiClock` reads a Wasm component's wall clock. A clock is a `Findable`
      value: it is an argument a program takes, not a global it reaches for.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import chronometries.unixChronometry

      val clock: Clock = Clock.fixed(Instant(12345L))
      (clock(), clock())   // (Instant(12345L), Instant(12345L))

      given Clock = clock
      now()                // Instant(12345L)
      ```
  refer aviation.now
  refer aviation.today
  refer aviation.MonotonicClock
  entry .current   the system wall clock, `System.currentTimeMillis`; the default
  entry .fixed   a clock which always reads one instant
  entry .offset   the system clock shifted by a `Duration`
      `Clock.offset(60*Second)` reads a minute ahead of the system clock, for exercising code
      against a time other than the present.
  entry #apply   the clock's reading, as an `Instant over Unix`

entry .Clockface   a time of day: hours, minutes, seconds and nanoseconds
  detail
      `Clockface(hour, minute, second, nanos)` is a time on a twenty-four-hour clock, whose
      hour is a `Base24` and whose minute and second are `Base60`s, so that
      `Clockface(14, 30, 59)` typechecks from literals and `Clockface(14, 60, 0)` does not;
      the seconds default to 0, as do the nanoseconds. `7.25.pm` is the literal form for an
      exact minute. A clockface has no date and no zone: `time on date` joins it to a date as a
      `Timestamp` (needing a `Calendar`), and `timestamp.time` reads it back.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import calendars.gregorianCalendar

      val time = Clockface(14, 30, 59)
      val appointment: Timestamp = time on 2024-Jan-15
      (time.hour, time.minute, time.second, time.nanos)   // (14, 30, 59, 0)
      ```

      `time.show` renders it with the `Clockface.Format`, `Numerics`, `Separation` and
      `Specificity` in scope — four givens, bundled by each of the `timeFormats` givens
      (`militaryTimeFormat` for `1430`, `civilianTimeFormat` for `02:30 PM`) or imported
      singly from `hourFormats`, `timeNumerics`, `timeSeparators` and `timeSpecificities`. A
      `Horology` adds hours, minutes or seconds to a clockface, wrapping at midnight.
  refer aviation.timeFormats
  refer aviation.Timestamp
  refer aviation.am
  entry .showable   renders a clockface from the `Format`, `Numerics`, `Separation` and `Specificity` in scope
      The hour is reduced to 1 to 12 when the `Format` is `halfDay`, padded to two digits
      under `Numerics.FixedWidth`, followed by the `Separation`'s separator and the two-digit
      minutes, then — when the `Format` says `seconds` — the second separator and two-digit
      seconds, and finally the `Format`'s postfix for the meridiem (` PM`, or nothing). The
      `Specificity` is required but does not alter the rendering.
  entry .Format   whether the clock has twelve hours, shows seconds, and what follows the time
    detail
        A `Format` answers `#halfDay` (twelve hours, 1 to 12, or twenty-four, 0 to 23),
        `#seconds` (whether they are shown) and `#postfix(meridiem)` (what follows the time:
        a space and the meridiem for a twelve-hour clock, nothing for a twenty-four-hour one).
        The `hourFormats` package has the four standard ones.
    entry #halfDay   whether hours run 1 to 12 with a meridiem rather than 0 to 23
    entry #seconds   whether the seconds are shown
    entry #postfix   the text after the time, given the meridiem: ` PM`, or nothing
  entry .Numerics   whether the hour is padded to two digits: `FixedWidth` or `VariableWidth`
      `timeNumerics.fixedWidthTimeNumerics` gives `02:30` and `variableWidthTimeNumerics`
      `2:30`; minutes and seconds are always two digits.
  entry .Specificity   whether a time is given to the minute or the second: `Minutes` or `Seconds`
      One of the four givens `Clockface.showable` needs, from `timeSpecificities`.
  entry .Separation   the text between hours and minutes, and between minutes and seconds
    detail
        `#separatorChar()` is the separator (`:`, `.`, `h` or nothing; the `timeSeparators`
        package has each), `#separator` the same as a value, and `#secondSeparator` — the
        same unless overridden — what precedes the seconds.
    entry #separatorChar   the separator, as text
    entry #separator   the separator, as text: `#separatorChar()`
    entry #secondSeparator   what precedes the seconds; the separator unless overridden
  entry #hour   the hour, 0 to 23
  entry #minute   the minute, 0 to 59
  entry #second   the second, 0 to 59
  entry #nanos   the nanoseconds within the second
  entry #on   this time on a date, as a `Timestamp`: `5.25.pm on 2018-Aug-11`
      Needs a `Calendar` in scope. The result is `Timestamp(date, this)`.

entry .Chronometry   how an `Instant over X`'s `Long` relates to atomic time: the meaning of a timeline
  detail
      A `Chronometry` is a typeclass over a timeline marker (`Tai`, `Unix`) which converts the
      timeline's ticks to and from TAI, International Atomic Time, the one strictly linear
      scale: `#toTai` and `#fromTai`. Any two timelines convert through TAI, which is how
      `instant.over[Tai]` works. `Chronometry.tai` is the identity; `Chronometry.unix`
      adds or removes the leap seconds in the `LeapSeconds` table. `Monotonic` has no
      chronometry, since the monotonic clock has no fixed relation to wall-clock time, and so
      a `Monotonic` instant cannot be converted or placed in a zone.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import chronometries.unixChronometry
      import instantDecodables.iso8601InstantDecodable
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val instant = t"2017-06-15T00:00:00Z".as[Instant over Unix]
      instant.over[Tai].long - instant.long     // 37000
      instant.over[Tai].over[Unix] == instant   // true
      Instant(0L).over[Tai] - Instant(0L)       // does not compile
      ```
  refer aviation.chronometries
  refer aviation.LeapSeconds
  refer aviation.Instant
  entry .Ambient   the timeline a bare `Instant(…)` takes, chosen from `chronometries`
    detail
        `Instant(millis)` and the instant decoders need a `Chronometry.Ambient` in scope, whose
        `#Transport` is the marker type the instant is `over` and whose `#chronometry` is that
        timeline's `Chronometry`. There is no default; `chronometries.unixChronometry` or
        `chronometries.taiChronometry` is imported by name.
    entry #Transport   the marker type of the chosen timeline: `Unix` or `Tai`
    entry #chronometry   the chosen timeline's `Chronometry`
  entry .tai   the chronometry of TAI: the identity
  entry .unix   the chronometry of POSIX time: leap seconds added to reach TAI, removed to return
      `toTai` is `LeapSeconds.tai` and `fromTai` is `LeapSeconds.untai`; a TAI value inside
      an inserted leap second, which has no Unix preimage, maps to the following second.
  entry #toTai   a timeline's tick count as TAI milliseconds
  entry #fromTai   TAI milliseconds as the timeline's tick count

entry .LeapSeconds   the table of leap seconds since 1972, and the arithmetic between POSIX time and TAI
  detail
      Since 1972, leap seconds have been inserted into UTC at the end of June or December to
      keep civil time within a second of the Earth's rotation; a `Unix` instant ignores them,
      so elapsed POSIX time between two instants is short by the leap seconds between. This
      object holds the table — one bit per half-year from 1972 to 2035, when leap seconds are
      to be abolished — and converts between the Unix and TAI millisecond timelines:
      `Chronometry.unix` is built on it. Before 1972 the TAI−UTC offset is taken as a constant
      ten seconds (an approximation; the true offset then was fractional and drifting), and
      after 2035 it stays at its final value, thirty-seven seconds.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      LeapSeconds.during(Year(1972), false)   // 10
      LeapSeconds.during(Year(1972), true)    // 11: the first leap second, 30 June 1972
      LeapSeconds.during(Year(2017), false)   // 37
      LeapSeconds.tai(0L)                     // 10000L
      LeapSeconds.untai(LeapSeconds.tai(1483228800000L))   // 1483228800000L
      ```
  refer aviation.Chronometry
  refer aviation.Tai
  entry .addLeapSecond   records a newly announced leap second, at the end of June or December of a year
      Sets the bit for the year (`midYear` for June, else December). Only insertions can be
      recorded: a negative leap second has never occurred and has no encoding.
  entry .during   the TAI−UTC offset in seconds in force in the first or second half of a year
      `during(year, false)` is the offset at the start of the year, and `during(year, true)`
      after June's possible leap second.
  entry .tai   POSIX milliseconds as TAI milliseconds: the leap seconds so far, added
  entry .untai   TAI milliseconds as POSIX milliseconds: the inverse of `.tai`
      A TAI value inside an inserted leap second has no POSIX counterpart and maps to the
      following second by convention; otherwise `untai(tai(t)) == t`.
  entry .smearTai   POSIX milliseconds as smeared TAI: each leap second spread over the day around it
      Google's "smear": rather than a discrete step, the offset grows linearly over the
      twenty-four hours centred on the leap instant, so there is no `23:59:60` and the clock
      runs at most a part in 86400 slow. Outside a smear window it agrees with `.tai`.
  entry .unsmearTai   smeared TAI milliseconds as POSIX milliseconds: the inverse of `.smearTai`
      Exact: `unsmearTai(smearTai(t)) == t`.

entry .LeapMode   whether sub-day arithmetic on a `Moment` counts leap seconds: `Lenient` or `Exact`
  detail
      `Lenient` adds a sub-day `Timespan` on the leap-free POSIX line, so two hours later is
      two hours of wall-clock time whatever happened between; `Exact` adds it as real SI
      seconds through TAI, so an inserted leap second crossed in between shifts the result
      back by a second. `LeapMode.lenient`, in the companion, is the default;
      `leapModes.exactLeapMode` is imported by name to switch.
  refer aviation.leapModes
  entry .lenient   `LeapMode.Lenient`, the default: leap seconds are stepped over

entry .GapPolicy   what a `Moment` inside a spring-forward gap grounds to
  detail
      When the clocks go forward, a span of wall-clock time never happens, and grounding a
      `Moment` in it to an `Instant` has two candidates: `forward`, the instant reached by
      keeping the offset in force before the transition, which lands just after the gap, and
      `backward`, the instant under the new offset, just before it. `#resolve` chooses one,
      or raises. `GapPolicy.pushForward`, the companion's default, chooses `forward`, as
      `java.time` does, so grounding is total without an import; `gapPolicies` has the
      alternatives. `Moment#instant`, `Timestamp#instant` and the `Moment` orderings all
      take a `GapPolicy`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import calendars.gregorianCalendar

      val spring = Moment(2024-Mar-31, Clockface(1, 30, 0), tz"Europe/London")
      spring.instant   // 01:30 UTC: pushed forward past the gap
      ```
  refer aviation.gapPolicies
  refer aviation.Moment
  entry .pushForward   the default: a time in the gap lands just after it
  entry #resolve   one of the two candidate instants, or an abort through a captured tactic

entry .Disambiguation   what a day past the end of a month becomes, when months or years are added to a date
  detail
      `2024-Jan-31 + 1*Month` asks for the 31st of February. `#resolve(year, month, day)` is
      given such a day, which may exceed the month's length in the `Calendar` passed to it,
      and returns a real `Date`: the last day of the month (`monthEnds.clampMonthEnd`), the
      day in the next month the excess reaches (`overflowMonthEnd`), or an abort through the
      `Tactic[Moment.Error]` the raising policy captured when it was imported
      (`raiseMonthEnd`). There is deliberately no default: month or year arithmetic on a date
      does not compile until one is imported by name.
  refer aviation.monthEnds
  entry #resolve   a real date for a year, month and possibly overflowing day, in a calendar

entry .Endianness   the order of a date's fields: `BigEndian`, `LittleEndian` or `MiddleEndian`
    `BigEndian` is year, month, day (ISO 8601); `LittleEndian` day, month, year (Europe);
    `MiddleEndian` month, day, year (the United States). One of the four givens a composed
    date rendering needs, imported from `dateEndianness` or bundled by `dateFormats`; an
    `Anniversary` and a `Monthstamp` read it too, treating anything but `LittleEndian` as
    month first.

entry .Frequency   the cadence of an `Rrule`: `Secondly` to `Yearly`
    RFC 5545's `FREQ`: `Secondly`, `Minutely`, `Hourly`, `Daily`, `Weekly`, `Monthly` or
    `Yearly`, which the rule's `interval` multiplies, so `Frequency.Weekly` with `interval =
    2` is every other week. A rule over a `Date` uses `Daily` and above; the sub-day
    frequencies apply to a rule over a `Timestamp`, where `byHour` and `byMinute` expand
    within the day.

entry .Hebdomad   which weekday starts the week, and which days are the weekend
  detail
      A `Hebdomad` (a seven-day week) answers `#start` and `#weekend(day)`, and from those
      numbers the days: `#weekday(ordinal)` is the day at a position from the week's start
      and `#ordinal(day)` the position of a day. It is consulted by `weekday.weekend`,
      `weekday.number`, `Weekday(ordinal)`, `date.weekend` and the working-day arithmetic
      `date + WorkingDays(n)`, and has no default: `hebdomads.europeanHebdomad`,
      `northAmericanHebdomad` or `jewishHebdomad` is imported by name, or one is defined.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import hebdomads.northAmericanHebdomad

      summon[Hebdomad].start   // Sun
      Weekday(Prim)            // Sun
      Sat.weekend              // true
      Mon.number               // Sec
      ```
  refer aviation.hebdomads
  refer aviation.Weekday
  refer aviation.WorkingDays
  entry #start   the weekday the week begins on
  entry #weekend   whether a weekday is part of the weekend
  entry #weekday   the weekday at an `Ordinal` position from the week's start
  entry #ordinal   the `Ordinal` position of a weekday from the week's start

entry .Holiday   a date which is a holiday, with an optional name
  detail
      `Holiday(2025-Dec-25, t"Christmas Day")`: a `Date` and an `Optional[Text]` name. A list
      of them makes a `Holidays`, and holidays compare by date.
  entry .comparable   orders holidays by their date
  entry #date   the date
  entry #name   the holiday's name, or `Unset`

entry .Holidays   the set of holidays a working-day calculation skips
  detail
      `Holidays(List(Holiday(…), …))` takes any collection `Traversable by Holiday` and indexes
      it by date, so that `#holiday(date)` finds the holiday on a date, `#workDay(date)` says
      whether a date is free of one, and `#between(start, end)` lists those from `start` up
      to but not including `end`, in date order. Bound as a given, together with a
      `Hebdomad`, it is what `date + WorkingDays(n)` and `date - WorkingDays(n)` consult.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import calendars.gregorianCalendar
      import hebdomads.europeanHebdomad

      given Holidays = Holidays(List
        ( Holiday(2025-Dec-25, t"Christmas Day"),
          Holiday(2025-Dec-26, t"Boxing Day") ))

      summon[Holidays].holiday(2025-Dec-25).let(_.name)   // Christmas Day
      summon[Holidays].workDay(2025-Dec-27)               // true: a Saturday, but not a holiday
      summon[Holidays].between(2025-Dec-1, 2026-Jan-1)    // both holidays
      2025-Dec-24 + WorkingDays(1)                        // 2025-Dec-29
      ```

      A holiday which falls on a weekend is not counted twice; working-day arithmetic skips
      the day once.
  refer aviation.Holiday
  refer aviation.WorkingDays
  entry .apply   a `Holidays` from any traversable collection of `Holiday`s
  entry #holiday   the holiday on a date, or `Unset`
  entry #workDay   whether a date has no holiday (weekends are the `Hebdomad`'s business)
  entry #between   the holidays from a start date up to, not including, an end date, in order

entry .Horology   how hours, minutes and seconds are added to a `Clockface`
  detail
      A `Horology` names the types of a clock's three fields (`#Primary`, `#Secondary`,
      `#Tertiary`) and adds a count of each to a `Clockface` with carry: `#addTertiary(time,
      30)` adds thirty seconds, carrying into the minutes and hours, which wrap at midnight.
      `Horology.sexagesimal`, in the companion, is the ordinary clock of twenty-four hours,
      sixty minutes and sixty seconds (`Base24`, `Base60`, `Base60`). Nothing else in the
      library consumes a `Horology`; it is a hook for a clock divided otherwise.
  entry .sexagesimal   the twenty-four-hour, sixty-minute, sixty-second clock
  entry #Primary   the type of the first field: `Base24` hours on the sexagesimal clock
  entry #Secondary   the type of the second field: `Base60` minutes
  entry #Tertiary   the type of the third field: `Base60` seconds
  entry #TimeRepr   the representation of a time; `Time` on the sexagesimal clock
  entry #addPrimary   a clockface some hours later, wrapping at midnight
  entry #addSecondary   a clockface some minutes later, carrying into the hours
  entry #addTertiary   a clockface some seconds later, carrying into the minutes and hours

entry .Iso8601   the ISO 8601 date-time format: a parser to `Instant over Unix`, and what it can object to
  detail
      A `Date.Format` named `ISO 8601`. `.parse(text)` reads a date-time to an `Instant over
      Unix`, raising `Moment.Error` under a `Tactic[Moment.Error]`; it is the function behind
      `instantDecodables.iso8601InstantDecodable`. It accepts a calendar date `2023-05-28`, a
      month `2012-11`, a week date `2023-W21-7`, each in the extended or the basic form
      (`20230528`, `2023W217`), optionally followed by `T` and a time to the hour, minute or
      second with a decimal fraction (`.` or `,`) on the last field, and then `Z`, an offset
      `+02:00` or `+0200`, or nothing (taken as UTC). A leap second `23:59:60` is accepted and
      lands on the following second, since the Unix timeline has no room for it.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Iso8601.parse(t"1994-11-06T08:49:37Z")          // Instant(784111777000L)
      Iso8601.parse(t"2023-05-28T14:30:59.123+02:00")
      Iso8601.parse(t"2023-W21-7T14:30:59Z")
      Iso8601.parse(t"2020-12-31")                    // midnight UTC
      ```

      A fault raises `Moment.Error(_.Format(text, Iso8601, offset)(issue))` (error 333), _`text`
      does not conform to format ISO 8601 (a digit was expected at 7)_, with the `.Issue`
      saying what the parser wanted at the offset. Errors in the date accrue and the time is
      not parsed after one, since the fields are variable-width and the cursor would be
      misaligned.
  refer aviation.instantDecodables
  refer aviation.Rfc1123
  refer aviation.Moment
  entry .Issue   what the parser expected and did not find
      `Expect(char)` (a particular character, such as `-` after a week number), `Digit`,
      `DigitOrDash`, `DigitOrDashOrW` (after the year), `DigitOrColon` and `Timezone` (a `Z`
      or an offset at the end). Each is `Communicable` through the anonymous given in the
      object, as _a digit or `-` was expected_, which is how `Moment.Error` reports it.
  entry .parse   an ISO 8601 date-time, date, month or week date as an `Instant over Unix`

entry .internal   the home of `Year`, `Day`, `Anniversary` and `WorkingDays`, and of the literal macros
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the object exists so that its opaque types have a stable home and
      the macros behind `tz"…"`, `ts"…"`, `dur"…"`, `rec"…"`, `7.25.am` and `2024-Jan-15`
      have somewhere to live. `Year`, `Day`, `Anniversary` and `WorkingDays` are exported from
      here to the `aviation` package, and their pages there have the full account; the
      companions' members are listed here.
  entry .Year   a calendar year, as an opaque `Int`; see `aviation.Year`
    detail
        The opaque type `Year = Int`, with `Year(2024)` to make one and `year()` to read it.
        The companion is an irregular `Radix`, so `2*Year` is a `Timespan of Year.type`.
    entry .apply   `Year(2024)`: a year from its number
    entry .multiplicable   `n*Year` as a `Timespan of Year.type`
    entry .inspectable   `2024ʸ`: the number with a suffix, so it does not read as an `Int`
    entry .showable   the bare number
    entry .addable   `Year(2024) + 5` is `Year(2029)`
    entry .subtractable   `Year(2024) - 5` is `Year(2019)`
    entry .decodable   `t"2024".as[Year]`, through the `Int` decoder in scope
    entry .orderable   `Year(1999) < Year(2000)`
  entry .Day   a day of the month, as an opaque `Int`; see `aviation.Day`
    detail
        The opaque type `Day = Int`, with `Day(15)` to make one and `day()` to read it. The
        companion is a regular `Radix`, so `3*Day` is a `Timespan of Day.type`.
    entry .apply   `Day(15)`: a day from its number
    entry .multiplicable   `n*Day` as a `Timespan of Day.type`
    entry .decodable   `t"15".as[Day]`, through the `Int` decoder in scope
    entry .inspectable   `15ᵈ`: the number with a suffix
    entry .showable   the bare number
  entry .WorkingDays   a count of working days, as an opaque `Int`; see `aviation.WorkingDays`
    detail
        The opaque type `WorkingDays = Int`, with `WorkingDays(5)` to make one and `days()`
        to read it.
    entry .apply   `WorkingDays(5)`: a count from its number
    entry .inspectable   `5ʷᵈ`: the number with a suffix
  entry .Anniversary   a month and a day with no year, as an opaque `Short`; see `aviation.Anniversary`
    detail
        The opaque type `Anniversary = Short`, packing the month's ordinal above the day.
        `Anniversary(Mar, Day(14))` makes one, as does `Mar - 14`; `anniversary.month` and
        `anniversary.day` read it, and `anniversary(year)` is that year's date, needing a
        `RomanCalendar` and a `.NonexistentLeapDay` policy.
    entry .NonexistentLeapDay   what the 29th of February is in a common year
      detail
          A policy with one method, `#round(year)`, the date to use when `(Feb - 29)(year)`
          does not exist. The three standard policies are in `nonexistentLeapDays`, and there
          is no default.
      entry #round   the date which stands for the 29th of February in a year without one
    entry .apply   `Anniversary(month, day)`: an anniversary from its parts
    entry .inspectable   ISO 8601's yearless date, `--03-14`, needing no context
    entry .showable   `14 March` or `March 14`, from the `Endianness`, `Months` and `Date.Separation` in scope
  entry .day   the day of an `Anniversary`
  entry .month   the month of an `Anniversary`
  entry .apply   `year()`, `day()` and `days()` read the number; `anniversary(year)` is that year's date
      Four extensions of the same name: on `Year` and `Day` (with the target names `yearValue`
      and `dayValue`), the underlying `Int`; on `WorkingDays`, the count; and on `Anniversary`,
      the date in the given year, through the `RomanCalendar` and the
      `Anniversary.NonexistentLeapDay` in scope.
  entry .tzInterpolator   the macro behind `tz"…"`: a `Timezone` whose name `java.time` knows
      Rejects a literal with substitutions, and a name `ZoneId.of` rejects, as _`name` is not a
      valid timezone identifier_.
  entry .tsInterpolator   the macro behind `ts"…"`: a year, month, date, timestamp or moment by the literal's precision
      Parses canonical ISO 8601 (a four-digit year, then optional month, day, time, fraction
      and zone) or RFC 1123, validates the date against the Gregorian calendar and the time
      against the clock, and splices the value whose type matches the precision; a leap second
      `:60` is spliced as a `Moment` at `:59` with `leap = Leap.Inserted`.
  entry .durInterpolator   the macro behind `dur"…"`: an ISO 8601 duration as a `Timespan`
  entry .recInterpolator   the macro behind `rec"…"`: an ISO 8601 repeating interval as a `Recurrence`
      Splits the literal at `/` into the repetition count, the start (parsed as `ts"…"`) and the
      period (parsed as `dur"…"`), underlining the failing segment, and splices a `Recurrence`
      whose start type follows the literal's precision.
  entry .parseDuration   the ISO 8601 duration parser shared by `dur"…"` and `Timespan`'s decoder
      `P[nY][nM][nW][nD][T[nH][nM][nS]]`, with at least one component, as a tuple of years,
      months, weeks, days, hours, minutes and (fractional) seconds, or a message.
  entry .monthstampMinus   the macro behind `2024-Jan-15`: a date validated as the code compiles
      Follows the inlined operands to their literals, identifies the `RomanCalendar` in scope
      as one of the standard ones (through a local alias if need be), validates the date and
      splices `Date.julianDay(n)`. A non-literal year, month or day, or an unrecognised
      calendar, halts with a message saying so and pointing to the calendar's `jdn`.
  entry .validTime   the macro behind `7.25.am` and `7.25.pm`: a `Clockface` from a literal
      Requires exactly two digits of minutes, minutes below 60 and an hour of at most 12, each
      with its own error, and adds twelve hours for `pm` (and for `12.xx.am`, none for
      `12.xx.pm`, so that noon and midnight come out right).
  entry .englishOrdinal   `1st`, `2nd`, `3rd`, `4th`, `11th`, `21st`: the suffix the English `Vernacular` uses

entry .protointernal   the home of `Instant` and `Duration`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the object which holds the opaque type `Instant`, the alias
      `Duration` and their companions and extensions, exported from here to the `aviation`
      package, whose `Instant` and `Duration` pages have the full account.
  entry .Instant   a point in physical time, as an opaque `Long`; see `aviation.Instant`
    detail
        The opaque type `Instant = Long`, whose timeline is the phantom `Transport` set with
        `over`. The companion holds the constructors and the arithmetic givens, each generic
        in the transport and taking the timeline's `Resolution` so that it works whatever the
        tick length — milliseconds for `Unix` and `Tai`, nanoseconds for `Monotonic`.
    entry .inspectable   `⧗784111777000`: the tick count behind an hourglass
        An inspection must be available with no context, and what the ticks mean is in the
        type, so only the raw count can be shown; the hourglass keeps it from reading as a
        `Long`.
    entry .Min   the earliest instant on a timeline, `Long.MinValue`
    entry .Max   the latest instant on a timeline, `Long.MaxValue`
    entry .of   `Instant.of[Unix](millis)`: an instant on a named timeline, from its ticks
    entry .apply   `Instant(millis)`: an instant on the `Chronometry.Ambient` timeline in scope
        Needs an ambient chronometry from `chronometries`; the result is `Instant over
        ambient.Transport`.
    entry .underlying   declares the erased representation, `Long`
    entry .generic   an instant on any timeline as an epoch `Long`, across the `Instants` domain
        The `Abstractable` and `Instantiable` which let an instant cross to and from other
        libraries' instant types; the `Long` is tagged with the transport, not converted.
    entry .orderable   `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=` between instants on the same timeline
    entry .comparable   orders instants on one timeline by their ticks
    entry .plus   `instant + duration`: any `Quantity` of time, converted to the timeline's ticks
    entry .minus   `instant - instant` on the same timeline, as a `Duration` in seconds
        Two instants on different timelines do not subtract; `over` converts one first.
    entry .minusDuration   `instant - duration`
  entry .Duration   a quantity of seconds; see `aviation.Duration`
    detail
        The alias `Duration = Quantity[Seconds[1]]`, with `Duration(millis)` to make one from
        milliseconds and `Duration.Error` for text which does not read as one.
    entry .apply   `Duration(1000L)`: a duration from milliseconds
    entry .generic   any quantity of time as nanoseconds, across the `Durations` domain
        The `Abstractable` and `Instantiable` for every `Quantity[units]` normalisable to
        seconds, which let a duration cross to and from other libraries' duration types.
    entry .Error   raised by `durationDecodables.compactDurationDecodable` for text which is not a duration
      detail
          Error 335: _`text` is not a recognized duration_. A negative, a word, an unknown
          unit or an empty text.
      entry #text   the text which was not a duration
  entry .long   an instant's tick count
  entry .over   `instant.over[Tai]`: the same instant on another timeline, through TAI
      Needs a `Chronometry` for both timelines, so a `Monotonic` instant cannot be converted.
  entry .~   `start ~ finish`: a half-open `Period` of instants
  entry .in   `instant.in(timezone)`: the instant as a `Moment` in a zone
      Needs a `RomanCalendar` and the timeline's `Chronometry`. The date is taken in the
      proleptic Gregorian calendar `java.time` counts in, and a moment on the later side of a
      fall-back overlap is marked `Occurrence.Second`, so that it grounds back to this instant.
  entry .timestamp   `instant.timestamp`: the instant as a zoneless `Timestamp` in the `Timezone` in scope
  entry .from   `duration.from(instant)`: the `Period` which starts at an instant and lasts the duration

entry .timestampInternal   the home of `Timestamp`, `Date` and `Monthstamp`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the object which holds the opaque type `Timestamp`, the aliases
      `Date = Timestamp in Day` and `Monthstamp = Timestamp in Month`, their companions, the
      givens for all three (which live in `Timestamp`'s companion so that they are in the
      implicit scope of the one underlying type) and the extensions. It is separate from
      `internal` so that its accessors do not collide after erasure with those of `Year` and
      `Day`. The `aviation` pages of `Timestamp`, `Date` and `Monthstamp` have the full
      account.
  entry .Timestamp   a date and a time of day as an opaque `Long`; see `aviation.Timestamp`
    detail
        The opaque type `Timestamp = Long`, packed as the Julian day number times the
        milliseconds in a day, plus the millisecond of the day; `Form` is the phantom
        precision. `Timestamp(date, time)` makes one.
    entry .apply   `Timestamp(date, time)`: a timestamp from a date and a clockface
    entry .inspectable   `2023-05-28T14:30:59`: ISO 8601 in the Gregorian calendar, needing no context
        Covers `Date` and `Monthstamp` too, which show their zeroed time of day; milliseconds
        appear only when non-zero.
    entry .timestampShowable   `time, date`, from the `Clockface is Showable` and `Date is Showable` in scope
    entry .timestampSubtractable   `timestamp - timestamp`: a `Timespan` of days, hours, minutes and seconds
        The civil difference, by truncated division, calendar-free; its type is `.Difference`.
    entry .timestampDecodable   `t"2024-01-15T14:30:59".as[Timestamp]`, raising `Timestamp.Error`
        Reads `yyyy-MM-dd` then a space or `T` then `HH:mm:ss`, in the Gregorian calendar,
        with the reason `BadFormat` for another shape, `BadNumber` for a field which is not
        an integer and `BadTime` for a date or time out of range.
    entry .dateUnderlying   declares `Date`'s erased representation, `Long`
    entry .dateSubtractable   `date - date`: the whole days between, as a `Quanta[Days[1]]`
    entry .dateSubtractable2   `date - days`: a date some `Quanta[Days[1]]` earlier
    entry .dateAddable   `date + days`: a date some `Quanta[Days[1]]` later
    entry .dateAddable2   `days + date`: the same, with the operands reversed
    entry .dateShowable   `date.show` from the `Endianness`, `Date.Numerics`, `Date.Separation` and `Years` in scope
        Reads the fields in the Gregorian calendar whatever calendar is in scope, orders them
        by the endianness, pads the month and day under `FixedWidth` and the year to two
        digits under `TwoDigitYear`, and joins them with the separator. The `dateFormats`
        givens bundle the four.
    entry .dateDecoder   `t"2011-12-13".as[Date]`: a `year-month-day` of digits, raising `Moment.Error`
        Cut at `-` into three integers and built in the Gregorian calendar; the month and day
        may be one or two digits. Text of another shape raises `Moment.Error(_.Format(…))`,
        and a month or day out of range `Moment.Error(_.Unknown(…))` or `_.Invalid(…)`.
    entry .dateEncodable   `date.encode`: `2024-01-15`, in the `RomanCalendar` in scope
    entry .dateOrderable   `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=` between dates
    entry .dateComparable   orders dates by their Julian day number
    entry .timestampOrderable   `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=` between timestamps
        `Orderable`'s `Self` is invariant, so `Date` and `Timestamp` each need an instance
        though the comparison is the same.
    entry .timestampComparable   orders timestamps on the millisecond grid
    entry .dateWorkingDays   `date + WorkingDays(n)`: the working day `n` working days on
        Needs a `Holidays` and a `Hebdomad`. Moves forward over weekend days and holidays,
        landing on a working day even for `WorkingDays(0)`.
    entry .dateWorkingDaysSubtractable   `date - WorkingDays(n)`: the working day `n` working days back
    entry .monthShowable   `monthstamp.show`: the month and year from the `Months`, `Date.Separation`, `Endianness` and `Years` in scope
        `2024/Jan` under `LittleEndian`, `Jan/2024` otherwise, with the separator between.
    entry .monthstampSubtractable   the result-type witness for `monthstamp - day`: a `Date`
        The operator's expansion always takes `.monthstampSubtraction`'s inline path, which
        checks the date as the code compiles; this `subtract` is reached only by a direct
        call, and a day the month does not have is a defect there.
    entry .monthstampSubtraction   the inline operator behind `2024-Jan-15`: `Monthstamp.MonthstampSubtraction`
    entry .monthstampAddable   `monthstamp + span`: the whole months and years of a `Timespan` added, staying month-precise
    entry .monthstampTimespanSubtractable   `monthstamp - span`: the whole months and years of a `Timespan` subtracted
    entry .Error   raised by `t"…".as[Timestamp]` for text which is not a timestamp
      detail
          Error 902: _the time `value` could not be parsed because `reason`_, with the reason's
          number as the error's second part: `BadFormat` (1), _the input did not match the
          expected timestamp format_; `BadNumber` (2), _a numeric component could not be
          parsed as an integer_; `BadTime` (3), _a date or time component was outside the
          valid range_.
      entry .Reason   `BadFormat`, `BadNumber` or `BadTime`
      entry .communicable   renders each reason as its message
      entry #value   the text which could not be parsed
      entry #reason   why
  entry .Date   the `Date` companion: `julianDay`, the checked constructor, and the rendering parts
    detail
        `Date` itself is the alias `Timestamp in Day`; this object makes dates and declares
        the types a date rendering is composed from.
    entry .julianDay   `Date.julianDay(2460325)`: a date from its Julian day number, unchecked
    entry .apply   `Date(year, month, day)` in the `Calendar` in scope, raising `Moment.Error` if it does not exist
        Inline, so that the `Tactic[Moment.Error]` resolves where it is called; the parameter
        types are the calendar's own `Annual`, `Mensual` and `Diurnal`, so the month must be
        the calendar's month type.
    entry .Format   a textual format a date-time may be parsed from, named for error messages
      detail
          `Iso8601` and `Rfc1123` extend it, each with its own `#Issue` type saying what the
          parser expected; `Moment.Error(_.Format(text, format, offset)(issue))` reports a
          fault against it.
      entry #name   the format's name: `ISO 8601`, `RFC 1123`
      entry #Issue   the format's type of objection, which must be `Communicable`
    entry .Numerics   whether a shown date's month and day are padded: `FixedWidth` or `VariableWidth`
    entry .Separation   the text between a shown date's fields
      detail
          `#separatorChar()` is the separator and `#separator` the same as a value; the
          `dateSeparators` package has `/`, `-`, `.` and a space.
      entry #separatorChar   the separator, as text
      entry #separator   the separator, as text: `#separatorChar()`
  entry .Difference   the type of `timestamp - timestamp`: a `Timespan` of days, hours, minutes and seconds
  entry .Monthstamp   the alias `Timestamp in Month`: a month-precise timestamp; see `aviation.Monthstamp`
      The type alone lives here, beside `Date`, so that its givens (`Timestamp.monthShowable`,
      `monthstampSubtractable`, `monthstampSubtraction`, `monthstampAddable` and
      `monthstampTimespanSubtractable`) are in the implicit scope of the underlying
      `Timestamp`; the `Monthstamp` object in the `aviation` package holds the factory and the
      compile-time `-` operator.
  entry .date   the date of a timestamp, its time of day dropped
  entry .time   the time of day of a timestamp, as a `Clockface`
  entry .jdn   the Julian day number of a timestamp's date
  entry .year   the year of a timestamp in the `Calendar` in scope; of a `Monthstamp`, in the Gregorian calendar with none
      Two extensions of the name: on `Timestamp` (and so on `Date`), `calendar.annual(date)`
      for the `Calendar` in scope; on `Monthstamp`, the Gregorian year, needing no calendar,
      since a monthstamp is tagged with the Gregorian `Month`.
  entry .month   the month of a timestamp in the `Calendar` in scope; of a `Monthstamp`, the Gregorian `Month`
      As `.year`: `calendar.mensual(date)` on a `Timestamp`, and the Gregorian month on a
      `Monthstamp` with no calendar needed.
  entry .day   the day of the month of a timestamp, in the `Calendar` in scope
  entry .weekday   the weekday of a timestamp's date, from its Julian day number
  entry .weekend   whether a timestamp's date is a weekend day, by the `Hebdomad` in scope
  entry .anniversary   the Gregorian month and day of a timestamp, as an `Anniversary`
  entry .monthstamp   the month of a timestamp, as a `Monthstamp`, in the `RomanCalendar` in scope
  entry .yearDay   the day of the year, from 1, in the `Calendar` in scope
  entry .hour   the hour of a timestamp's time
  entry .minute   the minute of a timestamp's time
  entry .second   the second of a timestamp's time
  entry .in   `timestamp.in(timezone)`: the timestamp as a `Moment` in a zone
  entry .stdlib   the timestamp as a `java.time.LocalDateTime`, in the `RomanCalendar` in scope
  entry .instant   `timestamp.instant`: grounded in the `Timezone` in scope, through a `Moment`
      Needs a `Timezone`, a `RomanCalendar` and a `GapPolicy`, so that a bare timestamp
      honours the same gap policy as a moment; with no overlap selector it grounds at the
      earlier offset, `Occurrence.First`.
  entry .>   `timestamp > timestamp`
  entry .<   `timestamp < timestamp`
  entry .<=   `timestamp <= timestamp`
  entry .>=   `timestamp >= timestamp`
  entry .addDays   `date.addDays(31)`: a date some whole days later, or earlier for a negative count

entry .Meridiem   the half of the day a twelve-hour clock reading belongs to: `Am` or `Pm`
  detail
      An enum of two cases, `Am` (before noon) and `Pm` (noon and after), which a twelve-hour
      `Clockface.Format` writes after the time. The companion's `.showable` renders `AM` and
      `PM`; the `timeMeridiems` package offers `upperMeridiem`, `lowerMeridiem`,
      `upperPunctuatedMeridiem` and `lowerPunctuatedMeridiem` (`a.m.`/`p.m.`) to import by name
      instead, which `hourFormats.twelveHourClock` picks up from scope.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Meridiem.Pm.show   // PM

      locally:
        import timeMeridiems.lowerMeridiem
        Meridiem.Pm.show   // pm
      ```
  entry .showable   renders the meridiem as `AM` or `PM`, in implicit scope

entry .Moment   a wall-clock reading in a timezone: a date, a time, a zone and how a DST overlap or leap second resolves
  detail
      A `Moment` is a `Timestamp` anchored to a `Timezone` — one particular reading of a clock
      in one particular place. Where a `Timestamp` is zoneless and an `Instant` is a point on a
      physical timeline, a `Moment` is the bridge between them: `#instant` grounds it to the
      `Unix` timeline through the zone's rules, and `Instant#in(timezone)` and
      `Timestamp#in(timezone)` build one.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import calendars.gregorianCalendar

      val moment = Moment(2024-Jan-1, Clockface(1, 0, 0), tz"UTC")
      moment.instant                 // 2024-01-01T01:00:00Z, as an Instant over Unix
      Instant(0L).in(tz"UTC").date   // 1970-Jan-1
      ```

      Two things make a wall-clock reading ambiguous, and a `Moment` carries a field for each.
      When clocks fall back an hour repeats, and `#occurrence` says whether the reading is the
      `Occurrence.First` (the earlier offset, the default) or `Occurrence.Second` instance of
      that hour; `Instant#in` sets it correctly for an instant in the overlap. When an inserted
      leap second makes a minute sixty-one seconds long, `#leap` is `Leap.Inserted` for the
      reading `23:59:60`, which is stored as `:59` and grounds to the following second. A
      reading that never happened — one in the hour clocks skip in spring — is not a field of
      the moment but a contextual `GapPolicy`: `GapPolicy.pushForward` is the default,
      `gapPolicies.pushBackwardGapPolicy` holds the old offset, and
      `gapPolicies.rejectGapPolicy` raises `Moment.Error` with reason `Gap`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import calendars.gregorianCalendar

      val clock = Clockface(1, 30, 0)
      val earlier = Moment(2024-Oct-27, clock, tz"Europe/London")
      val later = Moment(2024-Oct-27, clock, tz"Europe/London", Occurrence.Second)
      later.instant.long - earlier.instant.long   // 3600000
      ```

      Moments order, compare and subtract (`.orderable`, `.comparable`, `.subtractable`) by
      their grounded instant, so a `Second` occurrence sorts after a `First` and two zones
      compare on absolute time; each of these, and `#instant` itself, needs a `RomanCalendar`
      and a `GapPolicy` in scope. A `Timespan` added to a moment (`Timespan.momentAdd`) applies
      its days and months to the wall clock and its hours, minutes and seconds physically,
      so `+ 1*Day` keeps the same local time across a DST change and `+ 24*Hour` does not.
      `Rrule[Moment]` expands a recurrence rule in the start's zone, and the `ts"…"`
      interpolator builds a moment from a zoned ISO 8601 literal.
  refer aviation.Timestamp
  refer aviation.Instant
  refer aviation.Timezone
  refer aviation.GapPolicy
  refer aviation.Occurrence
  refer aviation.Leap
  entry .generic   a moment as the epoch milliseconds of its instant, across `Instants`
      The `Abstractable across Instants to Long` given, which lets a moment be handed to any
      API that takes a generic instant. It grounds the moment with `#instant`, so it needs a
      `RomanCalendar` in scope (and uses the default `GapPolicy`).
  entry .orderable   moments order by their grounded instant
      Needs a `RomanCalendar` and a `GapPolicy` in scope, since the comparison grounds both
      sides. The `Second` occurrence of a repeated hour is later than the `First`, and two
      moments in different zones compare on absolute time, not on their wall-clock fields.
  entry .comparable   the runtime `Comparable` for moments, by grounded instant
      The runtime counterpart of `.orderable`, used by sorting and by `Period[Moment]`; needs
      a `RomanCalendar` and a `GapPolicy` in scope.
  entry .subtractable   `later - earlier`: the physical `Duration` between two moments
      The difference of the two grounded instants, in seconds, so it honours DST (an hour
      which the clocks skipped is not counted) and, under the counting interpretation of the
      `Unix` timeline, leap seconds. Needs a `RomanCalendar` and a `GapPolicy`.
  entry .showable   renders a moment as a zoned ISO 8601 date-time, `2024-01-01T01:00:00Z`
      The form is `yyyy-MM-ddTHH:mm:ss` followed by the zone's offset at that instant
      (`Z`, `+01:00`, …); an inserted leap second renders as the sixty-first second, `:60`.
      Needs a `RomanCalendar` and a `GapPolicy` in scope to find the offset.
  entry .Error   raised when a date, time or moment is not valid
    detail
        Error 333: _the date was not valid because `reason`_, with the reason's number as the
        sub-code. It is the error of the whole date-and-time vocabulary, not only of `Moment`:
        `Date(year, month, day)`, `Month(index)`, `Calendar#jdn`, `WeekDate(…)`,
        `OrdinalCalendar(year, dayOfYear)`, `Rfc1123.parse` and the ISO 8601 instant decoder
        raise it, and a `Monthstamp - day` computed at runtime goes through `Calendar#jdn`,
        which raises it too. Built with `Moment.Error(_.Gap)`, which also captures the `Diagnostics`.

        The `.Reason` enum has four cases. `Format(text, format, offset)(issue)`, number 1:
        _`text` does not conform to format `name` (`issue` at `offset`)_, from a parser such
        as `Rfc1123` or `Iso8601`, where `issue` is that format's own `Issue` type.
        `Invalid(year, month, day, calendar)`, number 2: _`year`-`month`-`day` does not exist
        in the calendar `name`_. `Unknown(text, kind)`, number 3: _`text` is not a recognized
        `kind`_, for a month name or index which is not one. `Gap`, number 4: _the wall-clock
        time does not exist in its timezone (a spring-forward DST gap)_, raised only under
        `gapPolicies.rejectGapPolicy`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import calendars.gregorianCalendar
        import gapPolicies.rejectGapPolicy
        import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

        capture[Moment.Error]:
          Moment(2024-Mar-31, Clockface(1, 30, 0), tz"Europe/London").instant
        // Moment.Error(Moment.Error.Reason.Gap)
        ```
    entry .apply   `Moment.Error(_.Unknown(text, kind))`: builds an error from a reason chosen on `Reason`
    entry .Reason   why a date or time was rejected: `Format`, `Invalid`, `Unknown` or `Gap`
        Each case carries a `number` (1 to 4) which becomes the error's sub-code, and the enum
        is `Communicable`, so a reason renders as the message quoted on `Moment.Error`'s page.
        A `Format` reason's `issue` is a dependent field typed by the `Date.Format` which
        produced it, such as `Rfc1123.Issue`.
    entry #reason   the `Moment.Error.Reason` this error carries
  entry #date   the calendar date of the reading
  entry #time   the time of day of the reading, as a `Clockface`
  entry #timezone   the zone whose rules ground the reading
  entry #occurrence   which instance of a repeated hour this is: `Occurrence.First` (default) or `Second`
      Meaningful only in a fall-back overlap, when the same wall-clock hour occurs twice.
      `Instant#in` sets it to `Second` for an instant in the later, post-transition offset.
  entry #leap   whether the reading is an inserted leap second: `Leap.None` (default) or `Leap.Inserted`
      A reading of `23:59:60` is stored as `:59` with `Leap.Inserted`, which `#instant` grounds
      one second later on the leap-free `Unix` line and `#tai` one SI second before the
      following second. The `ts"…"` interpolator sets it for a `:60` literal.
  entry #instant   grounds the reading to the `Unix` timeline through the zone's rules
      Needs a `RomanCalendar` and a `GapPolicy` in scope. A reading in a fall-back overlap is
      resolved by `#occurrence`; one in a spring-forward gap by the `GapPolicy` — forward
      (keeping the old offset on the same wall-clock, so landing just after the gap) by
      default, backward with `gapPolicies.pushBackwardGapPolicy`, or raising `Moment.Error`
      with reason `Gap` under `gapPolicies.rejectGapPolicy`. An inserted leap second grounds
      to the second that follows it.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label The default policy pushes a reading in the gap forward
        code
            import soundness.*
            import calendars.gregorianCalendar

            val spring = Moment(2024-Mar-31, Clockface(1, 30, 0), tz"Europe/London")
            spring.instant   // 01:30 UTC, which London reads as 02:30 BST
      frame
        label Pushing backward keeps the pre-transition offset
        code
            import soundness.*
            import calendars.gregorianCalendar
            import gapPolicies.pushBackwardGapPolicy

            val spring = Moment(2024-Mar-31, Clockface(1, 30, 0), tz"Europe/London")
            spring.instant   // 00:30 UTC, which London reads as 00:30 GMT
      frame
        label Rejecting makes the gap an error
        code
            import soundness.*
            import calendars.gregorianCalendar
            import gapPolicies.rejectGapPolicy
            import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

            val spring = Moment(2024-Mar-31, Clockface(1, 30, 0), tz"Europe/London")
            capture[Moment.Error](spring.instant)   // Moment.Error(_.Gap)
      ##
      ```
  entry #tai   grounds the reading to the atomic `Tai` timeline, counting leap seconds
      As `#instant`, converted to TAI, except that an inserted leap second is exactly one SI
      second before the following second rather than sharing its instant. Needs a
      `RomanCalendar` and a `GapPolicy`.
  entry #timestamp   the reading without its zone, as a `Timestamp`

entry .MonotonicClock   the source of `monotonic()` readings: elapsed time from an arbitrary origin
  detail
      A `MonotonicClock` reads the system's monotonic clock, whose readings are `Instant over
      Monotonic`: they tick in nanoseconds (`Resolution.monotonic`), never go backwards, and
      measure from an origin which means nothing — so they can be subtracted to a `Duration`
      or ordered, but never converted to a date or placed in a zone. `monotonic()` reads the
      `MonotonicClock` given in scope; `.current` (the JVM's `System.nanoTime`) is in implicit
      scope, and a Wasm program imports `clocks.wasiMonotonicClock` to read
      `wasi:clocks/monotonic-clock` instead.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val t0 = monotonic()
      ((t0 + 90*Second) - t0).value   // 90.0
      (t0 + 1*Second).long - t0.long  // 1000000000
      ```

      It is `Findable`, so a `MonotonicClock` of its own is bound as a local given to make a
      timing deterministic.
  refer aviation.monotonic
  refer aviation.Clock
  refer aviation.Resolution
  entry .current   the JVM's monotonic clock, `System.nanoTime`, in implicit scope
  entry #apply   reads the clock as an `Instant over Monotonic`

entry .Month   the twelve months of the Roman calendars, `Jan` to `Dec`, which are also a radix of timespans
  detail
      `Month` is an enum of `Jan`, `Feb`, `Mar`, `Apr`, `May`, `Jun`, `Jul`, `Aug`, `Sep`,
      `Oct`, `Nov` and `Dec`, exported to the package so the cases are used bare. It is the
      month type of every `RomanCalendar` (Julian, Gregorian, the cutover regimes) and of an
      `OffsetCalendar`; the other calendars have month enums of their own.

      The companion is a `MonthRadix`, so `n*Month` is a `Timespan of Month.type` — a count
      of calendar months, which adds to a `Date` only with a `Disambiguation` in scope for the
      day-of-month overflow. A month also builds dates: `2024 - Jan` is a `Monthstamp`, and
      `Jan - 15` an `Anniversary`, so `2024-Jan-15` reads left to right as a `Date`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import calendars.gregorianCalendar
      import monthEnds.clampMonthEnd
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Month(2)                  // Feb
      Month(t"Mar").numerical   // 3
      2024-Jan-31 + 1*Month     // 2024-Feb-29
      ```

      How a month is *shown* is a choice: `.showable` needs a `Months` in scope, imported from
      the `monthFormats` package (`englishMonths`, `englishShortMonths`, `frenchMonths`,
      `numericMonths`, …).
  refer aviation.Months
  refer aviation.Monthstamp
  refer aviation.Anniversary
  refer aviation.Timespan
  entry .all   the twelve months in order, as an `Array`
  entry .apply   the month at a one-based index, or by its three-letter name
      `Month(3)` is `Mar`; `Month(t"Mar")` is the same, by the enum's case name. An index
      outside 1 to 12, or a name which is not a case, raises `Moment.Error` with reason
      `Unknown`.
  entry .unapply   extractors for a one-based index and for a case-insensitive name
      `case Month(month)` matches an `Int` from 1 to 12 or a `Text` such as `t"mar"` or
      `t"MAR"` (the name is lower-cased and capitalised before lookup).
  entry .multiplicable   `n*Month`: a timespan of `n` calendar months
      The `Int is Multiplicable by Month.type` given, in implicit scope, which makes `3*Month`
      a `Timespan of Month.type`.
  entry .monthOfYear   `year - month`: a `Monthstamp`, as `2024 - Jan`
      The `Int is Subtractable by Month to Monthstamp` given which types the `-` between a
      year and a month. The operator itself expands through `.monthOfYearOp`, so that
      `2024 - Jan - 15` can still see the literal year and month.
  entry .MonthOfYear   the inline form of `year - month`, which keeps the literals visible to `Monthstamp - day`
      Not usually named: `.monthOfYearOp` supplies it. After inlining, `2024 - Jan` is the
      literal construction `Monthstamp(Year(2024), Jan)`, which is what lets the
      `Monthstamp - day` operator check `2024-Feb-30` while the code compiles.
  entry .monthOfYearOp   the inline given of `.MonthOfYear`
  entry .showable   renders a month by the `Months` in scope
      Needs a `Months`, imported by name from `monthFormats`; there is no default, so a
      `Month` cannot be shown without choosing one.
  entry .subtractable   `month - day`: an `Anniversary`, as `Mar - 14`
      The `Month is Subtractable` given with `Operand = Int` and `Result = Anniversary`,
      which is how the day of `2024-Mar-14` attaches to its month. The day is not checked
      here: `Anniversary` is applied to a year before a date exists.
  entry #numerical   the month's one-based number, `Jan` being 1
  entry #offset   the days of the year before this month begins, in a common or a leap year
      `Feb.offset(false)` is 31; `Mar.offset(true)` is 60. The calendars use it to compute a
      Julian day number from a year, month and day.

entry .Months   a choice of month names: how a `Month` is shown
  detail
      A `Months` maps each `Month` to its name, and is the context `Month.showable` and the date
      formats need. None is in implicit scope: one is imported by name from the `monthFormats`
      package — `englishMonths` (`January`), `englishShortMonths` (`Jan`), `frenchMonths`,
      `germanMonths`, `spanishMonths`, `oneLetterAmbiguousMonths`, `numericMonths` (`1`) or
      `twoDigitMonths` (`01`) — or written as a lambda, since the trait has the single method
      `#name`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import monthFormats.englishShortMonths

      Mar.show   // Mar
      ```
  entry #name   the name of a month under this choice

entry .Monthstamp   a year and a month, `2024 - Jan`: a month-precise timestamp
  detail
      A `Monthstamp` is `Timestamp in Month` — the first instant of a Gregorian month, tagged
      with month precision, so two stamps of the same month are equal. It is written as a
      year minus a month, `2024 - Jan`, and is the middle of a date literal: `2024-Jan-15` is
      `(2024 - Jan) - 15`. Its `year` and `month` read back without a calendar in scope, and
      `Date#monthstamp` recovers one from a date.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import calendars.gregorianCalendar

      val month: Monthstamp = 2024 - Jan
      (month.year(), month.month)       // (2024, Jan)
      (2024 - Feb) - 29                 // 2024-Feb-29
      (2025-Mar-14).monthstamp.month    // Mar
      ```

      Subtracting a day of the month gives that day's `Date`, and the operator checks it while
      the code compiles: `2024-Feb-30` is a compile error, not a runtime one. For that, the
      year, month and day must all be literals (or a local `val` or `given` alias of a
      literal), and the `RomanCalendar` in scope must be one of `calendars.gregorianCalendar`,
      `julianCalendar`, `papalCutover` or `britishCutover` — anything else is itself a compile
      error naming the operand. A date computed at runtime is built with the calendar's
      `jdn(year, month, day)` under a `Tactic[Moment.Error]` instead.

      Arithmetic in whole months and years keeps the result month-precise: `(2024 - Nov) +
      3*Month` is `2025 - Feb`, `(2024 - Jun) + 1*Year` is `2025 - Jun`, and subtracting a
      `Timespan` rolls back the same way; days, hours and finer components of the span are
      ignored. A monthstamp shows (with a `Months`, a `Date.Separation`, an `Endianness` and a
      `Years` in scope) as `Mar.2024` or `2024.Mar`, by the endianness. These givens live with
      `Timestamp`'s, in the implicit scope of the underlying type.
  refer aviation.Timestamp
  refer aviation.Date
  refer aviation.Month
  refer aviation.Year
  entry .apply   `Monthstamp(year, month)`: the first day of the month, as a month-precise stamp
      Computes the Gregorian Julian day number of the first of the month; the `2024 - Jan`
      operator reaches this through `Month.monthOfYear`.
  entry .MonthstampSubtraction   the inline `-` which turns `monthstamp - day` into a `Date`, checked at compile time
      Not usually named: the `monthstampSubtraction` given supplies it. Its `op` is a macro
      which, when the year, month and day are literals and the `RomanCalendar` in scope is a
      known one, validates the date as the code compiles and halts on an invalid one such as
      `2012-Feb-30`; a non-literal operand, or an unrecognised calendar, is a compile error
      naming it. `Monthstamp.monthstampSubtractable`, the plain `Subtractable` witness,
      panics rather than raises if its `subtract` is called directly with a day the month
      does not have.

entry .OffsetCalendar   a calendar which shares a Roman calendar's months and days but numbers its years with an offset
  detail
      An `OffsetCalendar(base, offset, name)` is a `RomanCalendar`'s months and days under
      another year count: the Thai Buddhist calendar is the Gregorian with 543 added
      (`calendars.buddhistCalendar`), and the Minguo calendar of the Republic of China is the
      Gregorian less 1911 (`calendars.minguoCalendar`). Both are givens in the `calendars`
      package, imported by name; a new one is built from any `RomanCalendar` and an `Int`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import calendars.buddhistCalendar
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Date(Year(2567), Jan, Day(1)).jdn   // 2460311, the same day as Gregorian 2024-Jan-1
      ```

      It is `Enumerated[Month]`, so its `Mensual` is `Month` and its `MonthUnit` is
      `Month.type`: a `Timespan of Month.type` adds to a date under it. Every calendar
      operation (`daysInYear`, `daysInMonth`, `annual`, `mensual`, `diurnal`,
      `zerothDayOfYear`, `computeJdn`) translates the year by the offset and delegates to the
      base.
  refer aviation.RomanCalendar
  refer aviation.Calendar
  entry #name   the calendar's name, such as `Buddhist`
  entry #MonthUnit   the radix of month timespans this calendar accepts: `Month.type`

entry .OrdinalCalendar   the ISO 8601 ordinal-date calendar: a year and a day-of-year, with no months
  detail
      `OrdinalCalendar` is a `Calendar` object with a single vestigial month, `.Annus`, which
      spans the whole year, so a date in it is a year and a day number from 1 to 365 or 366 —
      day 247 of 2024. Year boundaries are the Gregorian calendar's. It is the given
      `calendars.ordinalCalendar`, imported by name, and under it `Date#day` is the day of the
      year and `format` renders `2024-247`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import calendars.gregorianCalendar
      import calendars.ordinalCalendar
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      OrdinalCalendar(Year(2024), 60)               // 2024-Feb-29
      OrdinalCalendar.format(2024-Dec-31)           // 2024-366
      ```

      Its `Mensual` and `MonthUnit` are both `Annus.type`, so no month timespan applies to a
      date under it; `Date#yearDay` gives the same day number under any calendar without
      switching to this one.
  refer aviation.Calendar
  refer aviation.WeekDate
  entry .Annus   the one month of the ordinal calendar, spanning the year
      A `MonthRadix` object; its `.annusShowable` renders it as `Annus`.
  entry .annusShowable   shows `Annus` as the text `Annus`
  entry .Mensual   the month type: `Annus.type`
  entry .MonthUnit   the radix of month timespans: `Annus.type`, so none applies
  entry .name   `Ordinal`
  entry .apply   `OrdinalCalendar(year, dayOfYear)`: the date of the `n`th day of a year
      Raises `Moment.Error` with reason `Invalid` when the day is outside 1 to the year's
      length. Needs a `Tactic[Moment.Error]`.
  entry .format   renders a date as `year-dayOfYear`, such as `2024-247`

entry .Period   a half-open range between two points of one kind: instants, dates, timestamps or moments
  detail
      `Period(start, finish)` is the interval from `start` inclusive to `finish` exclusive,
      over any point type: an `Instant over X`, a `Date`, a `Timestamp` or a `Moment`. Being
      half-open, consecutive periods tile a timeline without sharing a boundary, which is why
      `has(finish)` is false. Two instants bound one with `start ~ finish`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import calendars.gregorianCalendar

      val hour = Instant(0L) ~ Instant(3_600_000L)
      hour.duration.value           // 3600.0
      hour.has(Instant(0L))         // true
      hour.has(Instant(3_600_000L)) // false
      hour.segments(15*Minute)      // four periods of fifteen minutes
      Period(2024-Jan-1, 2024-Jan-5).by(1*Day)   // Chain(2024-Jan-1, …, 2024-Jan-4)
      ```

      What a period's length is, and what it can be stepped by, follow the point: instants
      differ by a `Duration` and step by a `Duration` or a regular `Timespan`; dates,
      timestamps and moments differ and step by a `Timespan` (an irregular span pulling in the
      `Calendar` and `Disambiguation` that `Date + Timespan` needs). The ordering operations
      (`#intersect`, `#union`, `has`, `by`, `segments`) take the point's runtime `Comparable`,
      which every aviation point has in its companion. A `Recurrent`'s `within(period)`
      selects the occurrences a period contains.
  refer aviation.Instant
  refer aviation.Timespan
  refer aviation.Recurrent
  entry .inclusive   `period.has(point)`: membership, with the start included and the finish excluded
      The `Inclusive` given: a point is in the period when `start <= point < finish`, by the
      point's `Comparable`.
  entry .segments   splits a period into consecutive sub-periods of a given length
      An extension on `Period`. `period.segments(length)` is the list of `Period`s from
      `start` stepping by `length` until `finish`; `partial` (true by default) keeps a short
      final segment when the length does not divide the period exactly, and `partial = false`
      drops it. The step is anything the point adds (`point is Addable by step to point`): a
      `Duration` or `Timespan` for instants, a `Timespan` for dates and timestamps.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      (Instant(0L) ~ Instant(3_600_000L)).segments(25*Minute).size                   // 3
      (Instant(0L) ~ Instant(3_600_000L)).segments(25*Minute, partial = false).size   // 2
      ```
  entry .by   the points of a period at a step, as a lazy `Chain`
      An extension on `Period`. `period.by(step)` is `start`, `start + step`, `start +
      2·step`, … while before `finish`, which is excluded. The chain is lazy, so a period of
      years sampled by the day costs nothing until its values are demanded, and `keep(n)` is
      cheap.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import calendars.gregorianCalendar

      Period(2024-Jan-1, 2030-Jan-1).by(1*Day).keep(3)   // Chain(2024-Jan-1, 2024-Jan-2, 2024-Jan-3)
      ```
  entry #start   the first point of the period, included
  entry #finish   the point the period ends before, excluded
  entry #duration   `finish - start`, typed by the point: a `Duration` for instants, a `Timespan` for the rest
      Needs the point's `Subtractable by` itself; for a `Moment` that means a `RomanCalendar`
      and a `GapPolicy` in scope.
  entry #intersect   the overlap of two periods, or `Unset` if they only touch or are disjoint
      Two periods whose only common point would be one's finish and the other's start do not
      intersect, since the finish is excluded. Needs the point's `Comparable`.
  entry #union   one period covering both, or the two unchanged as a `Set` if they do not overlap
      Needs the point's `Comparable`. Overlapping periods merge into
      `Period(min(starts), max(finishes))`; disjoint ones are returned as a set of two.

entry .PersianCalendar   the Persian (Solar Hijri) calendar, by Birashk's arithmetical 2820-year cycle
  detail
      A `Calendar` whose months are `PersianMonth`: the first six of 31 days, the next five of
      30, and Esfand of 29, or 30 in a leap year. Its epoch, 1 Farvardin 1, is Julian day
      1948321 (22 March 622 in the Julian calendar). Leap years follow the arithmetical
      2820-year cycle, which is a deterministic approximation of the official observational
      calendar (the true vernal equinox at 52.5° E) and may differ from it by a day around some
      equinoxes. The given is `calendars.persianCalendar`, imported by name.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import calendars.persianCalendar
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      persianCalendar.leapYear(Year(1399))                                   // true
      persianCalendar.format(Date(Year(1399), PersianMonth.Esfand, Day(30))) // 30 Esfand 1399
      ```

      Its `MonthUnit` is `PersianMonth.type`, so `n*PersianMonth` adds to a date under it and a
      `Timespan of Month.type` does not.
  refer aviation.PersianMonth
  refer aviation.Calendar
  entry #name   `Persian`
  entry #MonthUnit   the radix of month timespans this calendar accepts: `PersianMonth.type`
  entry #leapYear   whether a year has 366 days, by the 2820-year cycle
  entry #daysInMonth   31 for the first six months, 30 for the next five, and 29 or 30 for Esfand

entry .PersianMonth   the twelve months of the Persian calendar, `Farvardin` to `Esfand`
  detail
      An enum: `Farvardin`, `Ordibehesht`, `Khordad`, `Tir`, `Mordad`, `Shahrivar`, `Mehr`,
      `Aban`, `Azar`, `Dey`, `Bahman` and `Esfand`, the months of `PersianCalendar`. The
      companion is a `MonthRadix` distinct from `Month`, so `3*PersianMonth` is a `Timespan of
      PersianMonth.type`, which adds to a date only under a calendar whose `MonthUnit` is that
      radix — mixing a Gregorian month span with a Persian date is a compile error.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import calendars.persianCalendar
      import monthEnds.clampMonthEnd
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Date(Year(1399), PersianMonth.Esfand, Day(30)) + 1*PersianMonth   // 1400 Farvardin 30
      ```
  entry .multiply   `n*PersianMonth`: a timespan of `n` Persian months

entry .Recurrence   an ISO 8601 repeating interval, `R5/2024-01-01/P1D`: a start, a period and an optional count
  detail
      A `Recurrence` is an event which repeats at a fixed cadence from a start: `start`,
      `start + period`, `start + 2·period`, … for `repetitions` occurrences, or forever when
      `repetitions` is `Unset` (ISO's bare `R/`). The period keeps its type, so the occurrences
      add correctly — `1*Month` honours calendar months (and so needs a `Calendar` and a
      `Disambiguation` in scope to generate), `8*Hour` is a fixed physical span — which is why
      a recurrence is typed by both: `Recurrence of Date by (Timespan of Month.type)`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import calendars.gregorianCalendar

      Recurrence(2024-Jan-1, 1*Day, 3).occurrences      // Chain(2024-Jan-1, 2024-Jan-2, 2024-Jan-3)
      Recurrence(2024-Jan-1, 1*Week).until(2024-Jan-22) // Chain(2024-Jan-1, 2024-Jan-8, 2024-Jan-15)
      Recurrence(2024-Jan-1, 1*Day, 5).encode           // R5/2024-01-01/P1D
      ```

      It is `Recurrent` (`.recurrent`), so `occurrences`, `until`, `within` and `following`
      apply to it. Note the month-end behaviour: a monthly recurrence from the 31st drifts
      under `monthEnds.clampMonthEnd` (Jan 31, Feb 29, Mar 29, …) because each occurrence is
      added to the previous one, where an `Rrule` re-anchors to the start's day and skips the
      months which lack it. Text forms: `#encode` and `.decodable` are the ISO wire form, the
      `rec"…"` interpolator checks a literal while the code compiles, and `show` describes the
      recurrence in a language chosen by the `Locale` in scope.
  refer aviation.Recurrent
  refer aviation.Rrule
  refer aviation.RecurrenceSet
  refer aviation.Timespan
  entry .apply   `Recurrence(start, period, repetitions = Unset)`: a recurrence typed by its start and period
  entry .encodable   the ISO 8601 repeating-interval text, `R3/2024-01-01/P1M`
      `R`, the repetition count if any, the start's own encoding, and the period as an ISO
      8601 duration, separated by `/`. Needs the start type to be `Encodable in Text`.
  entry .inspectable   the same repeating-interval form, from the parts' inspections
      Available for any start type which is `Inspectable`, and needing none of the `Locale`
      context the `Showable`s do; this is what a test report prints.
  entry .decodable   parses `R[n]/<start>/<period>` as a recurrence of a named period type
      Parsing erases which radix the period was in, so the caller names it in the type:
      `text.as[Recurrence of Date by (Timespan of Month.type)]`. A mismatched radix type
      mis-reads the duration, so name the one the data uses. Needs tactics for
      `Recurrence.Error` (malformed text), `Moment.Error` (the start) and `Timespan.Error`
      (the period).

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import calendars.gregorianCalendar
      import monthEnds.clampMonthEnd
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      t"R3/2024-01-01/P1M".as[Recurrence of Date by (Timespan of Month.type)].occurrences
      // Chain(2024-Jan-1, 2024-Feb-1, 2024-Mar-1)
      ```
  entry .englishShowable   describes the recurrence in English: _every 2 weeks, 5 times from 2024-01-01_
      Needs a `Locale[en]` in scope and the start type to be `Showable` (a `Date` needs a
      date format). The French, German and Spanish givens are chosen by their locales.
  entry .frenchShowable   describes the recurrence in French, under a `Locale[fr]`
  entry .germanShowable   describes the recurrence in German, under a `Locale[de]`
  entry .spanishShowable   describes the recurrence in Spanish, under a `Locale[es]`
  entry .recurrent   the `Recurrent` instance: the occurrences are `start`, `start + period`, …
      Needs the start to be `Addable by` the period type (`point is Addable by span to
      point`), which for a date and a month span means a `Calendar` and a `Disambiguation`
      in scope. The stream is infinite when `repetitions` is `Unset`.
  entry .Error   raised when text is not a valid ISO 8601 repeating interval
    detail
        _the value `value` is not a valid ISO 8601 repeating interval_, raised by
        `.decodable` when the text does not have three `/`-separated parts or the first is
        not `R` followed by digits. A bad start or period raises `Moment.Error` or
        `Timespan.Error` instead.
    entry #value   the text which did not parse
  entry #start   the first occurrence
  entry #period   the step between occurrences, typed as it was built (`Timespan of Month.type`, …)
  entry #repetitions   how many occurrences there are, or `Unset` for an unbounded recurrence

entry .RecurrenceSet   an iCalendar recurrence set: merged occurrence streams, plus extra dates, minus exclusions
  detail
      `RecurrenceSet(include, rdates, exdates)` is RFC 5545's recurrence set: the union of
      one or more ascending occurrence streams (`include`, each typically some rule's
      `occurrences`), with explicit extra points (`rdates`, `RDATE`) added and excluded
      points (`exdates`, `EXDATE`) removed. It is itself `Recurrent`, so its `occurrences` is
      one ascending, de-duplicated `Chain`, and it composes with any other `Recurrent`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import calendars.gregorianCalendar

      val weekly = Rrule(2024-Jan-1, Frequency.Weekly, count = 4)

      RecurrenceSet(include = List(weekly.occurrences), rdates = List(2024-Jan-10),
          exdates = List(2024-Jan-15)).occurrences
      // Chain(2024-Jan-1, 2024-Jan-8, 2024-Jan-10, 2024-Jan-22)
      ```

      The merge is lazy — it forces only the heads of the streams it compares — so infinite
      rules may be included and bounded afterwards with `until` or `within`. Every included
      stream must already be ascending.
  refer aviation.Recurrent
  refer aviation.Rrule
  entry .recurrent   merges the included streams and `rdates`, drops duplicates and `exdates`
      Needs the point's `Comparable`, which every aviation point has in its companion.
  entry #include   the occurrence streams to merge, each ascending
  entry #rdates   points added to the set, in any order
  entry #exdates   points removed from the set

entry .Recurrent   the typeclass of anything which yields an ordered, lazy stream of occurrences
  detail
      `Recurrent` is the common interface of the recurrence models: `Recurrence` (ISO 8601
      fixed intervals), `Rrule` (RFC 5545 rules) and `RecurrenceSet` (their unions). Each
      generates its occurrences differently and needs different context to do so — an
      `Addable` for a `Recurrence`, a `RomanCalendar` for an `Rrule` — which is why this is a
      typeclass carried in a given rather than a base trait: the instance captures what it
      needs, and the combinators `occurrences`, `until`, `within` and `following` are written
      once over any `Recurrent`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import calendars.gregorianCalendar

      def nextThree[sequence: Recurrent](sequence: sequence) = sequence.occurrences.keep(3)

      nextThree(Recurrence(2024-Jan-1, 1*Week))
      nextThree(Rrule(2024-Jan-1, Frequency.Monthly, byDay = List(WeekdayOrdinal(Fri, -1))))
      ```

      Its `Topic` is the point type of the stream: `Date`, `Timestamp` or `Moment`.
  refer aviation.Recurrence
  refer aviation.Rrule
  refer aviation.RecurrenceSet
  refer aviation.occurrences
  entry #occurrences   the ascending, lazy `Chain` of a value's occurrences
      Infinite for an unbounded recurrence, so it is consumed with `keep`, `until` or `within`
      rather than converted to a `List` outright.

entry .Regime   the calendar in force as it changed through history: segments handing over at Julian day numbers
  detail
      A `Regime` is a `RomanCalendar` made of ordered `.Segment`s, each a calendar and the
      first date on which it governs. A date is located in whichever segment governs its
      Julian day number, so a regime is used exactly as a single calendar is, and dates either
      side of a cutover translate automatically. The two-segment case is the Julian-to-
      Gregorian hybrid: `calendars.papalCutover` switches on 15 October 1582 (the day after
      Julian 4 October) and `calendars.britishCutover` on 14 September 1752; both are
      imported by name.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      given Regime = calendars.papalCutover
      (1582-Oct-4).jdn    // 2299160, the last Julian day
      (1582-Oct-15).jdn   // 2299161, the first Gregorian day — the very next day
      1582-Oct-10         // does not compile
      ```

      The dates in the gap exist in neither adjacent calendar: a literal in the gap is a
      compile error, and a date computed at runtime is rejected by the regime's `jdn` with
      `Moment.Error` (reason `Invalid`), since `validDate` is more than a day-of-month range
      check here. A year's leap status is that of the calendar governing its midpoint.
  refer aviation.RomanCalendar
  refer aviation.Calendar
  entry .Segment   one calendar and the first date it governs
    detail
        `Regime.Segment(from, calendar)`: the regime applies `calendar` from `from` (a `Date`,
        compared by Julian day number) until the next segment's `from`. The last day of one
        calendar and the first of the next share a Julian day, so the hand-over day is valid
        under both.
    entry #from   the first date the segment governs
    entry #calendar   the `RomanCalendar` in force from that date
  entry .apply   `Regime(name, segments*)`: a regime from its segments, sorted by their start
  entry #leapYear   whether a year is leap under the calendar governing its midpoint
  entry #leapYearsSinceEpoch   the governing calendar's leap-year count, used to compute Julian day numbers
  entry #validDate   whether a date exists: it must be located by some segment, so cutover-gap dates are not valid

entry .Resolution   how many nanoseconds one tick of an `Instant over X` is
  detail
      Each timeline keeps its instants as a `Long` of ticks, and `Resolution` says what a tick
      is: the `Unix` and `Tai` timelines tick every millisecond (`nanos` = 1 000 000), and
      `Monotonic`, a `System.nanoTime` reading, every nanosecond. Instant arithmetic
      (`instant + 1*Second`, `instant - duration`) uses the timeline's `Resolution` to turn
      seconds into ticks, so a second advances the right count on any timeline. The three
      givens are in implicit scope; a new timeline supplies its own.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val t0 = monotonic()
      (Instant(0L) + 1*Second).long   // 1000
      (t0 + 1*Second).long - t0.long  // 1000000000
      ```
  refer aviation.Instant
  refer aviation.Monotonic
  entry .unix   the `Unix` timeline ticks in milliseconds
  entry .tai   the `Tai` timeline ticks in milliseconds
  entry .monotonic   the `Monotonic` timeline ticks in nanoseconds
  entry #nanos   the nanoseconds in one tick

entry .Rfc1123   the RFC 1123 date format of HTTP headers: `Sun, 06 Nov 1994 08:49:37 GMT`
  detail
      `Rfc1123` is a `Date.Format` named `RFC 1123`, the fixed-width form HTTP uses for
      `Date`, `Expires` and `Last-Modified` headers: a three-letter weekday, a comma, a
      two-digit day, a three-letter month, a four-digit year, `HH:mm:ss` and the literal
      `GMT`. `.parse` reads one to an `Instant over Unix`, and
      `instantDecodables.rfc1123InstantDecodable` makes it the decoder behind
      `text.as[Instant over Unix]` when imported by name (the alternative being the ISO 8601
      decoder).

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import instantDecodables.rfc1123InstantDecodable
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      t"Sun, 06 Nov 1994 08:49:37 GMT".as[Instant over Unix]   // Instant(784111777000L)
      ```

      A single-digit day (`Wed, 3 Jul 2002 …`) is not RFC 1123 and is rejected: the format is
      fixed-width. The weekday is checked for spelling but not for agreement with the date.
  refer aviation.Iso8601
  refer aviation.Moment
  entry .Issue   what went wrong at a position of the text
      An enum: `DayName(days*)`, _expected a day name (…)_, listing the names the text could
      still have been; `MonthName(months*)`, _expected a month name (…)_; `Expect(char)`,
      _expected `char`_, for a missing comma, space, colon or letter of `GMT`; and `Digit`,
      _expected a digit_. An `Issue` travels inside `Moment.Error.Reason.Format` with the
      offset it occurred at, and is `Communicable`.
  entry .parse   reads RFC 1123 text to an `Instant over Unix`, or raises `Moment.Error`
      Needs a `Tactic[Moment.Error]`. Every field is parsed as its own venture, so one pass
      over a bad timestamp reports every digit problem, as `Moment.Error(_.Format(text,
      Rfc1123, offset)(issue))`; the instant is built only from a clean parse, as the
      `Timestamp` in `tz"GMT"`.

entry .RomanCalendar   the family of calendars with the twelve Roman months: Julian, Gregorian, and their cutover regimes
  detail
      A `RomanCalendar` is an `Enumerated[Month]` calendar with the month lengths everyone
      knows — 31, 28 or 29, 31, 30, … — which differs from its siblings only in `#leapYear`
      and `#leapYearsSinceEpoch`; the rest of the `Calendar` interface (`annual`, `mensual`,
      `diurnal`, `computeJdn`, `zerothDayOfYear`, `daysInMonth`, `daysInYear`) is derived
      from those two. `calendars.julianCalendar` (every fourth year),
      `calendars.gregorianCalendar` (except centuries not divisible by 400) and the `Regime`s
      `papalCutover` and `britishCutover` are the instances, each a given imported by name,
      and none is in scope by default: a Gregorian date literal needs
      `import calendars.gregorianCalendar`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import calendars.gregorianCalendar

      gregorianCalendar.leapYear(Year(1900))   // false
      (2024-Feb-29).jdn
      ```

      Much of the library asks for a `RomanCalendar` specifically rather than any
      `Calendar`: `Moment#instant`, `Date#monthstamp`, `WeekDate`, `Rrule` expansion and
      `Instant#in` all work in a calendar with `Month`s. A new one is written by extending the
      class with a name and the two leap-year methods.
  refer aviation.Calendar
  refer aviation.Regime
  refer aviation.OffsetCalendar
  entry #name   the calendar's name, such as `Gregorian`
  entry #MonthUnit   the radix of month timespans this calendar accepts: `Month.type`
  entry #leapYear   whether a year has a 29th of February; abstract
  entry #leapYearsSinceEpoch   the number of leap years up to a year, from which `zerothDayOfYear` is computed; abstract
  entry #daysInMonth   31, 30, or 28 or 29 for February according to `#leapYear`
  entry #daysInYear   366 in a leap year, else 365

entry .WeekdayOrdinal   one `BYDAY` entry of a recurrence rule: a weekday, optionally with an ordinal
  detail
      `WeekdayOrdinal(weekday, ordinal = Unset)` is one element of `Rrule#byDay`. A bare weekday
      (`WeekdayOrdinal(Tue)`, RFC 5545 `TU`) selects every Tuesday in the period; with an
      ordinal it selects one — `WeekdayOrdinal(Mon, 3)` is the third Monday (`3MO`) and
      `WeekdayOrdinal(Fri, -1)` the last Friday (`-1FR`), counting back from the end. The
      ordinal is meaningful only under `Frequency.Monthly` and `Frequency.Yearly`; under
      `Weekly` and `Daily` a weekday acts as a filter and the ordinal is ignored.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import calendars.gregorianCalendar

      Rrule(2024-Jan-1, Frequency.Monthly, byDay = List(WeekdayOrdinal(Mon, 3)), count = 2).occurrences
      // Chain(2024-Jan-15, 2024-Feb-19)
      ```
  entry #weekday   the weekday selected
  entry #ordinal   which instance in the period, positive from the start or negative from the end, or `Unset` for every one

entry .Rrule   an iCalendar recurrence rule (RFC 5545 `RRULE`) with its start: "the third Monday of every month"
  detail
      An `Rrule[point]` is a recurrence rule and its `DTSTART` as one value: a `start`, a
      `Frequency` (`Secondly` to `Yearly`), an `interval`, a bound (`count` or `until`), and
      the `by*` fields which select within each period — `byMonth`, `byWeekNo`, `byYearDay`,
      `byMonthDay`, `byDay`, `byHour`, `byMinute`, `bySecond` and `bySetPos` — plus the
      `weekStart` that `Weekly` periods align to. Each `frequency`×`interval` period is
      expanded, the `by*` fields select its candidates, and `bySetPos` picks from them by
      position. The patterns a fixed-interval `Recurrence` cannot express — the last Friday,
      the fourth Thursday of November, every weekday — are rules.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label A monthly rule takes its day from the start
        code
            import soundness.*
            import calendars.gregorianCalendar

            Rrule(2024-Jan-1, Frequency.Monthly).occurrences.keep(3)
            // Chain(2024-Jan-1, 2024-Feb-1, 2024-Mar-1)
      frame
        label A bare weekday selects every one in each month
        code
            import soundness.*
            import calendars.gregorianCalendar

            Rrule(2024-Jan-1, Frequency.Monthly, byDay = List(WeekdayOrdinal(Fri))).occurrences.keep(3)
            // Chain(2024-Jan-5, 2024-Jan-12, 2024-Jan-19)
      frame
        label An ordinal of -1 is the last one in each month
        code
            import soundness.*
            import calendars.gregorianCalendar

            Rrule(2024-Jan-1, Frequency.Monthly, byDay = List(WeekdayOrdinal(Fri, -1))).occurrences.keep(3)
            // Chain(2024-Jan-26, 2024-Feb-23, 2024-Mar-29)
      frame
        label An interval widens the period to every second month
        code
            import soundness.*
            import calendars.gregorianCalendar

            Rrule(2024-Jan-1, Frequency.Monthly, interval = 2, byDay = List(WeekdayOrdinal(Fri, -1)))
            . occurrences.keep(3)
            // Chain(2024-Jan-26, 2024-Mar-29, 2024-May-31)
      frame
        label A count bounds the rule, and the whole renders as RFC 5545 text
        code
            import soundness.*
            import calendars.gregorianCalendar

            val rule = Rrule(2024-Jan-1, Frequency.Monthly, interval = 2, count = 2,
                byDay = List(WeekdayOrdinal(Fri, -1)))

            rule.occurrences   // Chain(2024-Jan-26, 2024-Mar-29)
            rule.encode        // FREQ=MONTHLY;INTERVAL=2;COUNT=2;BYDAY=-1FR
      ##
      ```

      The point type decides what is generated. An `Rrule[Date]` yields dates; an
      `Rrule[Timestamp]` yields zoneless date-times, with `byHour`, `byMinute` and `bySecond`
      expanding each day (defaulting to the start's time) and the sub-day frequencies
      stepping the clock; an `Rrule[Moment]` expands in the start's timezone and grounds each
      occurrence there. All three are `Recurrent` through `RomanCalendar`-dependent givens,
      so `occurrences`, `until`, `within` and `following` need a calendar in scope.

      A date the month lacks is skipped, and the day re-anchors to the start: `Monthly` from
      the 31st of January yields Jan 31, Mar 31, May 31, …, as RFC 5545 says, and
      deliberately unlike a `Recurrence`, which drifts under a month-end policy. `until` is
      inclusive. Negative values count from the end throughout: `byMonthDay = List(-1)` is the
      last day of the month, `byYearDay = List(-1)` the last of the year, `byWeekNo = List(-1)`
      the last ISO week, `bySetPos = List(-1)` the last candidate of each period.

      `.parse` reads the RFC 5545 text of a rule and reattaches a start; `encode` writes it
      (without the start, as iCalendar keeps `DTSTART` separately); `inspect` includes the
      start; and `show` describes the rule in prose — _every month on the 3rd Monday_ — in
      the language of the `Locale` in scope, which also needs that language's `Months` and
      `Weekdays`.
  refer aviation.Recurrent
  refer aviation.WeekdayOrdinal
  refer aviation.Frequency
  refer aviation.Recurrence
  refer aviation.RecurrenceSet
  entry .dateRecurrent   an `Rrule[Date]` is `Recurrent`, yielding dates
      Needs a `RomanCalendar` in scope (and `Date`'s `Comparable`, which is in implicit
      scope). The stream is ascending, bounded by `count` and the inclusive `until`.
  entry .timestampRecurrent   an `Rrule[Timestamp]` is `Recurrent`, yielding zoneless date-times
      Needs a `RomanCalendar`. Under `Hourly`, `Minutely` and `Secondly` the clock is stepped
      by the interval, carrying whole days into the date, and the `by*` fields filter; under
      the date frequencies each date is expanded by `byHour`, `byMinute` and `bySecond`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import calendars.gregorianCalendar

      Rrule(Timestamp(2024-Jan-1, Clockface(0, 0, 0)), Frequency.Daily, byHour = List(9, 17),
          count = 3).occurrences
      // 09:00 and 17:00 on 1 January, then 09:00 on 2 January
      ```
  entry .momentRecurrent   an `Rrule[Moment]` is `Recurrent`, grounding each occurrence in the start's timezone
      Needs a `RomanCalendar` and, to compare moments, a `GapPolicy` (the default
      `GapPolicy.pushForward` suffices). The rule is expanded on the start's zoneless
      timestamp, and each result is placed `in` the start's `Timezone`, so a daily rule keeps
      its wall-clock time across a DST change.
  entry .encodable   renders the rule as RFC 5545 text, `FREQ=MONTHLY;INTERVAL=2;COUNT=10;BYDAY=3MO`
      Only the fields which differ from their defaults are written (`INTERVAL` when not 1,
      `WKST` when not `MO`), in the RFC's order; an `UNTIL` bound is written with the point's
      own `encode`, so the point type must be `Encodable in Text`. The start is not part of
      the text, as iCalendar carries `DTSTART` separately: `.parse` takes it back as an
      argument.
  entry .inspectable   the rule with its start, `Rrule(2024-Jan-1 ╱ FREQ=MONTHLY;BYDAY=3MO)`
      Needs only an `Inspectable` point, and none of the `Locale`, `Months` or `Weekdays` the
      `Showable`s need, so it is available wherever the rule is; this is what a test report
      prints.
  entry .parse   reads RFC 5545 rule text and attaches a start
      `Rrule.parse(t"FREQ=MONTHLY;BYDAY=-1FR", 2024-Jan-1)` is an `Rrule[Date]`; the start's
      type decides the rule's, and an `UNTIL` value is decoded as that type. Keys are
      case-insensitive; an unknown frequency, a malformed number or weekday code, or a
      missing `FREQ` raises `Rrule.Error`, so a `Tactic[Rrule.Error]` is needed. Unknown keys
      are ignored.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import calendars.gregorianCalendar
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Rrule.parse(t"FREQ=MONTHLY;BYDAY=-1FR", 2024-Jan-1).occurrences.keep(2)
      // Chain(2024-Jan-26, 2024-Feb-23)
      ```
  entry .englishShowable   describes the rule in English: _every year on the 4th Thursday of November_
      Needs a `Locale[en]`, a `Months` and a `Weekdays` in scope — all imported by name
      (`monthFormats.englishMonths`, `weekdays.englishWeekdays`, and `given Locale[en] =
      Locale(en)`) — so a rule cannot be described in a language whose names are not
      available. The French, German and Spanish givens are selected by their locales.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import calendars.gregorianCalendar
      import monthFormats.englishMonths
      import weekdays.englishWeekdays

      given Locale[en] = Locale(en)

      Rrule(2024-Jan-1, Frequency.Yearly, byMonth = List(Nov), byDay = List(WeekdayOrdinal(Thu, 4))).show
      // every year on the 4th Thursday of November
      ```
  entry .frenchShowable   describes the rule in French, under a `Locale[fr]`: _tous les mois le 3e lundi_
      Needs `monthFormats.frenchMonths` and `weekdays.frenchWeekdays` in scope as well.
  entry .germanShowable   describes the rule in German, under a `Locale[de]`
      Needs `monthFormats.germanMonths` and `weekdays.germanWeekdays` in scope as well.
  entry .spanishShowable   describes the rule in Spanish, under a `Locale[es]`
      Needs `monthFormats.spanishMonths` and `weekdays.spanishWeekdays` in scope as well.
  entry .Error   raised when text is not a valid RFC 5545 recurrence rule
    detail
        _the value `value` is not a valid RFC 5545 recurrence rule_, raised by `.parse` for a
        missing or unknown `FREQ`, a number or weekday code which does not parse, or a
        malformed `BYDAY` entry.
    entry #value   the rule text which did not parse
  entry #start   the first candidate occurrence, iCalendar's `DTSTART`; the rule's point type is its type
  entry #frequency   the period the rule repeats over: `Secondly`, `Minutely`, `Hourly`, `Daily`, `Weekly`, `Monthly` or `Yearly`
  entry #interval   how many periods between occurrences, 1 by default
  entry #count   the number of occurrences, or `Unset` for no limit
  entry #until   the last point an occurrence may fall on, inclusive, or `Unset`
  entry #byMonth   the months to occur in, as `Month`s; empty for all
  entry #byMonthDay   the days of the month, positive from the 1st or negative from the end
  entry #byDay   the weekdays, each optionally with an ordinal, as `WeekdayOrdinal`s
  entry #byYearDay   the days of the year, 1 to 366, or negative from the end; under `Yearly`
  entry #byWeekNo   the ISO week numbers, or negative from the last week; under `Yearly`
  entry #byHour   the hours of the day an occurrence falls at; for `Rrule[Timestamp]` and `Rrule[Moment]`
  entry #byMinute   the minutes of the hour, likewise
  entry #bySecond   the seconds of the minute, likewise
  entry #bySetPos   positions to keep from each period's candidates, 1-based or negative from the end
      `bySetPos = List(-1)` with `byDay` naming the five weekdays is the last weekday of the
      month.
  entry #weekStart   the weekday a `Weekly` period begins on, `Mon` by default (RFC 5545 `WKST`)

entry .Timespan   a calendar quantity of time: years, months, weeks, days, hours, minutes and seconds, each kept apart
  detail
      A `Timespan` is elapsed time as a vector of counts in each radix — `years`, `months`,
      `weeks`, `days`, `hours`, `minutes` — plus a physical `seconds` component, a
      `Quantity[Seconds[1]]`. It is distinct from a `Duration`, which is seconds alone: a
      month is not a fixed number of seconds and a day is not always twenty-four hours, so
      the counts are never carried into one another until the span is applied to a date, a
      timestamp or a moment, where the calendar and the zone decide what they mean.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import calendars.gregorianCalendar
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val span = 2*Year + 3*Month + 14*Day
      (span.years, span.months, span.days)   // (2, 3, 14)
      span.encode                            // P2Y3M14D
      t"P1Y2M3DT4H5M6S".as[Timespan]         // raises Timespan.Error on malformed text
      dur"PT36H"                             // checked while the code compiles
      ```

      Which radices a span carries is in its type: `n*Month` is a `Timespan of Month.type`,
      `3*Month + 2*Week` a `Timespan of (Month.type & Week.type)`, and `+`, `-` and `*n`
      intersect the operands' radix sets. That type decides what the span can be added to. A
      *regular* span — weeks, days, hours, minutes, seconds, but no month or year — is a
      definite physical duration, so it adds to an `Instant` on any timeline and converts
      across `Durations`. A span with months needs a `Calendar` whose `MonthUnit` is that
      month radix and a `Disambiguation` (`monthEnds.clampMonthEnd`, `overflowMonthEnd` or
      `raiseMonthEnd`) for a day the target month lacks; with years alone, a `Calendar` and
      a `Disambiguation` but no month radix constraint. Adding Persian months to a Gregorian
      date, or an irregular span to an `Instant`, is a compile error.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import calendars.gregorianCalendar
      import monthEnds.clampMonthEnd

      2024-Jan-1 + 3*Day                              // 2024-Jan-4, no policy needed
      2024-Jan-31 + 1*Month                           // 2024-Feb-29, clamped
      Instant(0L) + (1*Hour + 30*Minute)              // Instant(5400000L)
      Timestamp(2024-Jan-1, Clockface(23, 30, 0)) + 1*Hour   // 2024-Jan-2 at 00:30
      ```

      Added to a `Moment`, a span splits at the day: its years, months, weeks and days move
      the wall clock, so `+ 1*Day` is the same local time tomorrow whatever the clocks did
      overnight, while its hours, minutes and seconds are applied physically through the
      zone, so `+ 24*Hour` across a spring-forward is an hour later on the wall. The
      `LeapMode` in scope says whether those physical seconds count leap seconds.

      The text forms: `encode` and `inspect` are the ISO 8601 duration (`P1Y2M3DT4H5M6S`,
      `PT0S` for the empty span), `as[Timespan]` parses one, `dur"…"` checks a literal, and
      `show` — opt-in by importing `timespanFormats.englishRelativeTimespan` or its French,
      German or Spanish siblings, with a `Locale` — reads the span relative to now: _in 18
      minutes_, _8 minutes ago_, _just now_.
  refer aviation.Duration
  refer aviation.Date
  refer aviation.Moment
  refer aviation.Disambiguation
  refer aviation.dur
  entry .apply   `Timespan(years = 1, days = 3)` by named counts, or `Timespan(radix, n)` for a single radix
      The case-class constructor takes any of `years`, `months`, `weeks`, `days`, `hours`,
      `minutes` and `seconds` by name, defaulting to zero. `Timespan(Month, 3)` is the
      single-radix builder behind `3*Month`, typed `Timespan of Month.type`.
  entry .inspectable   the ISO 8601 duration, `P1Y2M3DT4H5M6S`, as the inspection form
      Self-identifying and complete (a component not written is zero), and needing none of
      the `Locale` context the relative `Showable` does; covers the refined `Timespan of X`
      types.
  entry .encodable   the ISO 8601 duration as the encoded form
      Zero components are omitted, the all-zero span is `PT0S`, weeks are written `W`
      alongside the other date components, and fractional seconds keep a decimal point.
  entry .decodable   parses an ISO 8601 duration, raising `Timespan.Error` on anything else
      `t"P1Y2M3DT4H5M6S".as[Timespan]` needs a `Tactic[Timespan.Error]`. The same parser
      backs the `dur"…"` interpolator at compile time.
  entry .interpolable   the `dur"P1Y2M3DT4H5M6S"` literal, validated as the code compiles
      Reached through the `dur` extension on `StringContext`; a malformed duration is a compile
      error.
  entry .Error   raised when text is not an ISO 8601 duration
    detail
        Error 334: _`text` is not a recognized duration_, raised by `.decodable`. A duration
        is not a moment, so it has an error of its own rather than a `Moment.Error` reason.
    entry #text   the text which did not parse
  entry .addable   `span + span`: adds each component, with the radix sets intersected in the type
  entry .subtractable   `span - span`: subtracts each component, with the radix sets intersected in the type
  entry .multiplicable   `span*n`: every component scaled by an `Int`
  entry .quantityAddable   `span + quantity`: folds a physical quantity of seconds into the span
      Takes any `Quantity` normalisable to seconds (`3*Second`, `500*Milli(Second)`), and
      adds `Seconds[1]` to the span's radix set.
  entry .quantitySubtractable   `span - quantity`: removes a physical quantity of seconds from the span
  entry .generic   a regular span as nanoseconds across `Durations`, in both directions
      The `Abstractable & Instantiable across Durations` given, for spans with no month or
      year radix: weeks and days count as 7 and 1 days of 86 400 seconds. A span built from
      nanoseconds comes back as seconds alone.
  entry .dateRegular   `date + span` for a span with no month or year: a fixed number of whole days
      Weeks count seven days; hours, minutes and seconds do not move a date. Needs no
      calendar or policy.
  entry .dateYear   `date + span` for a span of years but no months, under any `Calendar` and a `Disambiguation`
      Advances whole years, keeping the month, and resolves a day the target year lacks (29
      February) through the `Disambiguation` in scope, then adds the days and weeks.
  entry .dateMonths   `date + span` for a span of months, under the `Calendar` whose `MonthUnit` is that radix
      Needs a `Calendar` in scope whose `MonthUnit` is the span's month radix — so Gregorian
      months add only to a Gregorian date, Persian months to a Persian one — and a
      `Disambiguation`. Years are added first, then months one at a time, wrapping at each
      year's own month count (so the Hebrew calendar's variable year works), then the day of
      month is checked: a day the target month lacks, or one in a regime's cutover gap, is
      resolved by the policy — clamped to the month's end under `monthEnds.clampMonthEnd`,
      carried into the next month under `overflowMonthEnd`, or raised as `Moment.Error` under
      `raiseMonthEnd`. Finally the days and weeks are added.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import calendars.gregorianCalendar
      import monthEnds.overflowMonthEnd

      2024-Jan-31 + 1*Month   // 2024-Mar-2
      ```
  entry .instantPlus   `instant + span` for a regular span, on any timeline with a `Resolution`
      The span is its physical seconds (weeks and days at 86 400 each), scaled to the
      timeline's ticks. A span with months or years does not add to an instant: add it to a
      date or a moment.
  entry .instantMinus   `instant - span` for a regular span, on any timeline with a `Resolution`
  entry .timestampAdd   `timestamp + span`: the date part through `Date`'s addition, the time part rolled through the clock
      Needs whatever `Date is Addable by` the span needs — nothing for a regular span, a
      `Calendar` and a `Disambiguation` for months or years. Hours, minutes and seconds are
      added to the time of day and whole days carried into the date, so `23:30 + 1*Hour` is
      `00:30` the next day.
  entry .momentAdd   `moment + span`: calendar days on the wall clock, sub-day time through the zone
      Needs the `Timestamp` addition for the span, a `RomanCalendar` and a `LeapMode`
      (`LeapMode.lenient` is in implicit scope; `leapModes.exactLeapMode` counts leap
      seconds). The years, months, weeks and days are applied to the wall-clock timestamp and
      re-zoned; the hours, minutes and seconds are then added to the grounded instant and the
      result placed back in the zone. So a day across a DST change keeps its local time,
      twenty-four hours do not, and a day which lands in a spring-forward gap is resolved by
      the `GapPolicy` in scope.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label A day keeps the wall-clock time; twenty-four hours keep the elapsed time
        code
            import soundness.*
            import calendars.gregorianCalendar

            val saturday = Moment(2024-Mar-30, Clockface(12, 0, 0), tz"Europe/London")
            (saturday + 1*Day).time     // Clockface(12, 0, 0), on Sunday in BST
            (saturday + 24*Hour).time   // Clockface(13, 0, 0)
      frame
        label A day which lands in the gap is pushed forward by default
        code
            import soundness.*
            import calendars.gregorianCalendar

            val night = Moment(2024-Mar-30, Clockface(1, 30, 0), tz"Europe/London")
            (night + 1*Day).time   // Clockface(2, 30, 0): 01:30 on 31 March never happened
      frame
        label Or held back, with the pre-transition offset
        code
            import soundness.*
            import calendars.gregorianCalendar
            import gapPolicies.pushBackwardGapPolicy

            val night = Moment(2024-Mar-30, Clockface(1, 30, 0), tz"Europe/London")
            (night + 1*Day).time   // Clockface(0, 30, 0)
      frame
        label Or rejected
        code
            import soundness.*
            import calendars.gregorianCalendar
            import gapPolicies.rejectGapPolicy
            import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

            val night = Moment(2024-Mar-30, Clockface(1, 30, 0), tz"Europe/London")
            capture[Moment.Error](night + 1*Day)   // Moment.Error(_.Gap)
      ##
      ```
  entry #years   the count of years
  entry #months   the count of months, in whichever month radix the type records
  entry #weeks   the count of weeks
  entry #days   the count of days
  entry #hours   the count of hours
  entry #minutes   the count of minutes
  entry #seconds   the physical seconds, as a `Quantity[Seconds[1]]`, which may be fractional
  entry #Topic   the radices the span carries, as a type intersection such as `Month.type & Week.type`
      Phantom: the counts are all stored whatever the type says, but the type gates which
      additions are available. `Radix.Irregular` (months and years) excludes a span from
      instant arithmetic.

entry .Timezone   a named zone of the tz database, `Europe/London`, checked when the code compiles by `tz"…"`
  detail
      A `Timezone` is a place's rules for civil time, named as the IANA tz database names it:
      `Europe/London`, `America/New_York`, `UTC`. It is what a `Moment` carries and what
      `Instant#in` and `Timestamp#in` take, and it is also the context `today()` and
      `Timestamp#instant` read. The only ways to make one check the name: the `tz"…"`
      interpolator while the code compiles, and `Timezone(name)` or `text.as[Timezone]` at
      runtime, raising `Timezone.Error` for a name the database does not have.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import calendars.gregorianCalendar
      import instantDecodables.iso8601InstantDecodable
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val london: Timezone = tz"Europe/London"
      t"2024-07-15T12:00:00Z".as[Instant over Unix].in(london).time.hour   // 13
      Timezone(t"NotARealZone")   // raises Timezone.Error
      tz"NotARealZone"            // does not compile
      ```

      The zone's rules are those of the platform's `java.time` zone database; `Tzdb` parses
      the database's own source files for programs which need a newer one.
  refer aviation.Moment
  refer aviation.tz
  refer aviation.Tzdb
  entry .apply   `Timezone(name)`: a zone by name, or `Timezone.Error` if there is none
      Needs a `Tactic[Timezone.Error]`; the same as `name.as[Timezone]`.
  entry .unsafeFrom   wraps a name without checking it; for the `tz"…"` and `ts"…"` macros
      Takes the `Unsafe` capability. The macros have already validated the name at expansion,
      so the generated code re-checks nothing; ordinary code uses `.apply`.
  entry .decodable   `text.as[Timezone]`, raising `Timezone.Error` for an unknown name
      Checks the name with `ZoneId.of`; needs a `Tactic[Timezone.Error]`.
  entry .interpolable   the `tz"Europe/London"` literal, checked against the zone database as the code compiles
      Reached through the `tz` extension on `StringContext`; the name must be a literal with
      no substitutions, and an unknown one is a compile error.
  entry .Error   raised for a name which is not a zone in the database
    detail
        Error 929: _the name `name` does not refer to a known timezone_, raised by
        `Timezone(name)` and `text.as[Timezone]`.
    entry #name   the name which was not a zone
  entry #name   the zone's tz database name, as text
  entry #stdlib   the zone as a `java.time.ZoneId`, for Java APIs which take one

entry .Tzdb   a parser for the tz database's own source files: `Rule`, `Zone`, `Link` and `Leap` lines
  detail
      The rules behind every `Timezone` come from the IANA tz database, and `Tzdb` reads its
      source files (`europe`, `northamerica`, …) directly rather than through a platform API,
      so a database newer than the platform's can be loaded when a government changes its
      rules at short notice. `.parse` takes a file's lines and returns its `.Entry`s: a
      `Rule` with its `from`/`to` years, `MonthDate` (`lastSun`, `Sun>=8`, `15`), time of
      change, `save` and `letters`; a `Zone` with its sequence of `ZoneInfo` periods; a
      `Link` alias; and a `Leap` second. `.parseFile` reads a file bundled on the classpath
      under `aviation/tzdb/`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import logging.silentLogging

      val lines = Chain(t"Zone\tAmerica/Nowhere\t-5:00\t-\tEST")
      Tzdb.parse(t"northamerica", lines)
      // List(Tzdb.Entry.Zone(America, Nowhere, …))
      ```

      A line the format does not permit raises `Tzdb.Error`, naming the fault and its line
      number, rather than yielding a zone with the wrong rules; parsing logs `Tzdb.Event`.
      The entries are data: nothing here yet turns them into a `Timezone`'s rules.
  refer aviation.Timezone
  entry .Time   a time of day in a tz line: hours, minutes, seconds and an optional suffix
    detail
        `Tzdb.Time(hours, minutes, seconds, suffix)`: the `AT` field of a `Rule` or the time
        of a `Leap`. The suffix, if any, is `s` for local standard time or `u` for UT, as the
        database writes `2:00s` and `1:00u`; absent, the time is local wall-clock time.
    entry #hours   the hour, 0 to 23
    entry #minutes   the minute
    entry #seconds   the second
    entry #suffix   `s` (standard time), `u` (universal time), or `Unset` for wall-clock time
  entry .Duration   a signed offset in a tz line: the `STDOFF` of a zone or the `SAVE` of a rule
    detail
        `Tzdb.Duration(hours, minutes, seconds)`. West of Greenwich the offset is negative,
        and the sign applies to every component, so `-0:30` is `Duration(0, -30, 0)`. A
        `SAVE` may exceed twenty-four hours.
    entry #hours   the hours, signed
    entry #minutes   the minutes, with the same sign
    entry #seconds   the seconds, with the same sign
  entry .ZoneInfo   one period of a zone's history: its standard offset, rules, format and end
    detail
        A `Zone` line and each continuation line beneath it is a `ZoneInfo`: the standard
        offset from UT, the name of the `Rule` set (or `-`) in force, the abbreviation format,
        and the `UNTIL` text at which the next period takes over (`Unset` for the current
        one).
    entry #stdoff   the standard offset from UT, as a `Tzdb.Duration`
    entry #rules   the name of the rules in force, or `-` for none
    entry #format   the abbreviation, as a function of the rule's letters: `E%sT` applied to `S` is `EST`
    entry #until   the `UNTIL` text of the period, or `Unset` for the last
  entry .MonthDate   the day a rule takes effect: an exact day, the last weekday, or a weekday on or after/before a day
    detail
        An enum of the database's `ON` field forms: `Exact(month, day)` for `15`;
        `Last(month, weekday)` for `lastSun`; `After(month, weekday, date)` for `Sun>=8`,
        the first Sunday on or after the 8th; and `Before(month, weekday, date)` for
        `Sun<=25`, the last Sunday on or before the 25th.
  entry .Entry   one line of a tz source file: a `Rule`, a `Zone`, a `Link` or a `Leap`
    detail
        The enum `.parse` returns a `List` of. A `Zone` gathers its continuation lines into
        one entry; the others are one line each.
    entry .Rule   a daylight-saving rule: when the clocks change, by how much, and in which years
      entry #name   the rule set's name, which a `ZoneInfo#rules` refers to
      entry #from   the first year the rule applies
      entry #end   the last year, `Int.MaxValue` for `max`, or the same as `from` for `only`
      entry #change   the day of the change, as a `Tzdb.MonthDate`
      entry #time   the time of day of the change, as a `Tzdb.Time`
      entry #save   the amount saved, as a `Tzdb.Duration`
      entry #letters   the letters substituted into a zone's format, or `Unset` for `-`
    entry .Leap   a leap second: its date, time and whether it was added or removed
      entry #year   the year
      entry #month   the month
      entry #day   the day of the month
      entry #time   the time of day
      entry #addition   true for an inserted second (`+`), false for a removed one
    entry .Zone   a zone and the sequence of periods in its history
      entry #area   the part of the name before the `/`, such as `America`
      entry #location   the part after the `/`, such as `New_York`, or `Unset` for a one-part name
      entry #info   the zone's periods in order, as a `Sequence[Tzdb.ZoneInfo]`
    entry .Link   an alias: `from` is the target zone, `to` the alternative name
      entry #from   the zone linked to
      entry #to   the name which is an alias for it
  entry .parseFile   parses a tz source file bundled on the classpath under `aviation/tzdb/`
      `Tzdb.parseFile(t"europe")` reads the resource `/aviation/tzdb/europe` through the
      class's own classloader and parses it with `.parse`. Needs a `Tactic[Tzdb.Error]` —
      raised with reason `NoTzdbFile` if the resource is absent — and a `Tzdb.Event is
      Loggable`.
  entry .parse   parses the lines of a tz source file into entries
      `Tzdb.parse(name, lines)` takes the file's name (for the log) and its lines as a
      `Chain[Text]`, and returns the `List` of `Tzdb.Entry`s. Comments after `#` are
      dropped; a `Rule`, `Link` or `Leap` line is one entry, a `Zone` line opens an entry
      which its indented continuation lines extend. Needs a `Tactic[Tzdb.Error]`, raised for
      any field that does not parse, and a `Tzdb.Event is Loggable`, such as
      `logging.silentLogging`.
  entry .Error   raised when a line of the database cannot be parsed
    detail
        Error 385: _the timezone could not be parsed at line `line`: `reason`_, with the
        reason's number as the sub-code. The `.Reason`s: `CouldNotParseTime(time)` (1),
        _could not parse time_, for a malformed `AT`, `STDOFF` or `SAVE`; `UnexpectedRule`
        (2), a `Rule` or `Leap` line with the wrong fields; `UnexpectedLink` (3);
        `UnexpectedZoneInfo` (4), a continuation line with no `Zone` above it;
        `UnparsableDate` (5), an `ON` field which is none of the known forms;
        `BadZoneInfo(line)` (6); `BadName(name)` (7), a zone name with more than one `/`; and
        `NoTzdbFile(name)` (8), _the zonefile `name` could not be found on the classpath_,
        from `.parseFile`.
    entry .communicable   renders each reason as its message
    entry .Reason   why a line failed, numbered 1 to 8
    entry #reason   the `Tzdb.Error.Reason`
    entry #line   the one-based line number of the fault
  entry .Event   what parsing logs
    detail
        `ParseTzdb(name)`, _parsing the timezone database file `name`_, logged at `fine` when
        `.parse` begins. Tagged `Log.Time`.
    entry .communicable   renders the event as its message
    entry .ParseTzdb   a database file is being parsed
      entry #name   the file's name

entry .TimeUnit   the seven units a duration or a frequency is counted in, coarsest first
    An enum: `Years`, `Months`, `Weeks`, `Days`, `Hours`, `Minutes`, `Seconds`. A
    `Vernacular` names them in its language — `Vernacular.components(span)` is a timespan as
    `(count, unit)` pairs, and `Vernacular.unitOf(frequency)` the unit an `Rrule`'s
    `Frequency` repeats in.

entry .Vernacular   one language's words and grammar for describing timespans and recurrences in prose
  detail
      A `Vernacular` turns a `Timespan`, a `Recurrence` or an `Rrule` into a sentence: _in 18
      minutes_, _every 2 weeks, 5 times from 2024-01-01_, _every month on the 3rd Monday_.
      The structure — which clauses apply and in what order — is shared, in the final
      methods `#relativeTimespan`, `#recurrence` and `#rrule`; each language supplies the
      words and the grammatical hooks (`#units`, `#everyUnit`, `#onDays`, …). `.english`,
      `.french`, `.german` and `.spanish` are the four provided, and are what the `Showable`
      givens of `Rrule`, `Recurrence` and `timespanFormats` pick by `Locale`. They are a first
      pass intended for refinement by native speakers. A new language implements the trait's
      abstract methods.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import calendars.gregorianCalendar
      import monthFormats.englishMonths
      import weekdays.englishWeekdays

      Vernacular.english.relativeTimespan(Timespan(minutes = -8))   // 8 minutes ago
      Vernacular.english.rrule(Rrule(2024-Jan-1, Frequency.Monthly, byMonthDay = List(-1)))
      // every month on the last day
      ```
  refer aviation.Rrule
  refer aviation.Recurrence
  refer aviation.Timespan
  entry .components   a timespan's non-zero components as `(count, TimeUnit)` pairs, coarsest first
  entry .unitOf   the `TimeUnit` a `Frequency` repeats in: `Monthly` is `Months`
  entry .english   the English vernacular: _every year on the 4th Thursday of November_
  entry .french   the French vernacular: _tous les mois le 3e lundi_
  entry .german   the German vernacular: _jeden Monat am 3. Montag_
  entry .spanish   the Spanish vernacular: _cada mes el tercer lunes_
  entry #units   a count with its unit's singular or plural name, _1 month_, _3 months_
  entry #conjunction   the word joining the last two items of a list: _and_
  entry #position   an ordinal position, with negatives from the end: _3rd_, _last_, _2nd-to-last_
  entry #justNow   the description of an empty timespan: _just now_
  entry #future   wraps a positive span as a time ahead: _in 18 minutes_
  entry #past   wraps a negative span as a time behind: _8 minutes ago_
  entry #everyDuration   the cadence of a `Recurrence`: _every 2 weeks_
  entry #everyUnit   the cadence of an `Rrule`: _every month_, _every 3 days_
  entry #times   a repetition count: _, 5 times_
  entry #from   the start of a recurrence: _from 2024-01-01_
  entry #onDays   the `byDay` clause from `(ordinal, weekday name)` pairs: _on the 3rd Monday_
  entry #onMonthDays   the `byMonthDay` clause: _on the 1st and last day_
  entry #inMonths   the `byMonth` clause: _in November_, or _of November_ after a day clause
  entry #takingPositions   the `bySetPos` clause: _taking the last_
  entry #relativeTimespan   a timespan relative to now, forward or back by its sign; the shared assembly
      _in 18 minutes_ for a positive span, _8 minutes ago_ for a negative one, `#justNow`
      for an empty one; this is what `timespanFormats.englishRelativeTimespan` calls.
  entry #recurrence   an ISO 8601 recurrence's description: cadence, count and start
      `recurrence(period, repetitions, start)`, with the start already rendered as text:
      _every 2 weeks, 5 times from 2024-01-01_.
  entry #rrule   an `Rrule`'s description: cadence, day clause, month clause, positions and count
      Needs a `Months` and a `Weekdays` in scope to name the rule's months and weekdays,
      which is why the `Rrule` `Showable`s ask for them.

entry .WeekDate   ISO 8601 week dates: a date as a week-year, a week number and a weekday
  detail
      ISO 8601 labels a date not by month but by `(week-year, week, weekday)`: weeks run
      Monday to Sunday, week 1 is the week containing 4 January (the first Thursday of the
      year), and a date near the year boundary may belong to the previous or the next
      week-year — 1 January 2000, a Saturday, is week 52 of 1999. This is not a `Calendar`
      (it has no months) but a labelling of a Gregorian date, so the `RomanCalendar` is
      contextual.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import calendars.gregorianCalendar
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      (WeekDate.weekYear(2000-Jan-1)(), WeekDate.weekOfYear(2000-Jan-1))   // (1999, 52)
      WeekDate(Year(1999), 52, Sat)                                         // 2000-Jan-1
      WeekDate.weekOfYear(2004-Dec-31)                                      // 53
      ```

      `Rrule#byWeekNo` selects dates by these week numbers.
  refer aviation.Date
  refer aviation.Weekday
  entry .weekYear   the ISO week-year a date belongs to, which may differ from its calendar year
      Needs a `RomanCalendar` in scope.
  entry .weekOfYear   the ISO week number of a date, 1 to 52 or 53
      Needs a `RomanCalendar` in scope.
  entry .apply   `WeekDate(weekYear, week, weekday)`: the date with those ISO coordinates
      Counts from the Monday of week 1. Needs a `RomanCalendar` and a `Tactic[Moment.Error]`,
      which the construction of 4 January in the week-year may raise.

entry .Weekday   the seven days of the week, `Mon` to `Sun`
  detail
      An enum of `Mon`, `Tue`, `Wed`, `Thu`, `Fri`, `Sat` and `Sun`, exported to the package
      so the cases are used bare. `Date#weekday` is a date's weekday, `WeekdayOrdinal` pairs
      one with an ordinal for a recurrence rule, and `Tzdb.MonthDate` uses them in the tz
      database's `lastSun` forms.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import calendars.gregorianCalendar
      import hebdomads.europeanHebdomad
      import weekdays.englishWeekdays

      (2024-Jan-1).weekday                        // Mon
      Sat.weekend                                 // true
      Weekday.count(2025-Mar-1, 2025-Apr-1, Sat)  // 5
      Sun.show                                    // Sunday
      ```

      The enum's `ordinal` runs from `Mon` = 0, but which day begins the week, and which are
      the weekend, is a `Hebdomad` — `hebdomads.europeanHebdomad`, `northAmericanHebdomad` or
      `jewishHebdomad`, imported by name — which `#number`, `#weekend` and `.apply` consult.
      How a weekday is shown is a `Weekdays`, from the `weekdays` package.
  refer aviation.Weekdays
  refer aviation.Hebdomad
  refer aviation.WeekdayOrdinal
  entry .apply   the weekday at an ordinal position of the week, under the `Hebdomad` in scope
      `Weekday(Prim)` is `Mon` under `hebdomads.europeanHebdomad` and `Sun` under
      `northAmericanHebdomad`.
  entry .all   the seven weekdays in order, as an `Array`
  entry .showable   renders a weekday by the `Weekdays` in scope
      Needs a `Weekdays`, imported by name from the `weekdays` package; there is no default.
  entry .count   how many of a given weekday fall in a half-open range of dates
      `Weekday.count(start, end, day)` counts the `day`s from `start` inclusive to `end`
      exclusive: the Saturdays in March 2025 are `Weekday.count(2025-Mar-1, 2025-Apr-1, Sat)`,
      which is 5.
  entry #weekend   whether the day is part of the weekend under the `Hebdomad` in scope
  entry #number   the day's one-based position in the week under the `Hebdomad` in scope, as an `Ordinal`

entry .Weekdays   a choice of weekday names: how a `Weekday` is shown
  detail
      A `Weekdays` maps each `Weekday` to its name, the context `Weekday.showable` needs and
      the `Rrule` descriptions use. None is in implicit scope: one is imported by name from the
      `weekdays` package — `englishWeekdays` (`Monday`), `englishShortWeekdays` (`Mon`),
      `twoLetterWeekdays` (`Mo`), `oneLetterAmbiguousWeekdays` (`M`, `T`, `W`, `T`, …),
      `shortestUnambiguousWeekdays` (`M`, `Tu`, `W`, `Th`, …), `frenchWeekdays`,
      `germanWeekdays` or `spanishWeekdays` — or written as a lambda, since the trait has the
      single method `#name`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import weekdays.englishShortWeekdays

      Wed.show   // Wed
      ```
  entry #name   the name of a weekday under this choice

entry .Years   how a year is written in a date: two digits or four
    An enum of `TwoDigitYear` and `FullYear`, the choice the `Date` and `Monthstamp`
    `Showable`s take from scope. `yearFormats.fullYears` and `yearFormats.twoDigitsYears` are
    the givens, imported by name; `2024 - Mar` shows as `Mar.2024` under one and `Mar.24`
    under the other (with a `Months`, a `Date.Separation` and an `Endianness` also in scope).

entry .WasiClockApi   the type of the WASI clocks interface: `Interface in Wit at "/aviation/clocks.wit"`
    The WIT definitions the Wasm clock givens are typechecked against, and which the `call`
    materialiser consults for each function's module id. `wasiApis.wasiClockApi` is its one
    value. Part of `aviation.wasi`.

entry .wasiApis   the WASI interface definition the Wasm clocks are compiled against
  detail
      In `aviation.wasi`, for programs compiled to a Wasm component. The given here is the
      `WasiClockApi` the `clocks` givens summon when they expand, so a Wasm program imports it
      by name alongside the clock it wants (`import wasiApis.wasiClockApi` and
      `import clocks.wasiClock`), and the `/aviation/clocks.wit` resource bundled with the
      module must be on the classpath at that site. The summoning site must be compiled for a
      Wasm target, since the inline given expands to a component import.
  entry .wasiClockApi   the `wasi:clocks` WIT interface, loaded from the bundled `/aviation/clocks.wit`

entry .clocks   clocks backed by `wasi:clocks`, for a program compiled to a Wasm component
  detail
      In `aviation.wasi`. On the JVM the default `Clock.current` and
      `MonotonicClock.current` are in implicit scope and nothing need be imported; a Wasm
      component has no `System.currentTimeMillis`, so it imports one of these givens by name,
      with `wasiApis.wasiClockApi`, and `now()`, `today()` and `monotonic()` then read the
      WASI host's clocks. Both givens are `inline`, so the Wasm component import materialises
      at the importing site, which must itself be compiled for a Wasm target; the compiler's
      note that the anonymous class is duplicated at each inline site is expected, and a
      WASI-linked application summons each once.
  refer aviation.Clock
  refer aviation.MonotonicClock
  entry .wasiClock   a `Clock` reading `wasi:clocks/wall-clock` `now`
      The host's `datetime` record of seconds and nanoseconds is converted to the `Unix`
      timeline's millisecond ticks. Needs `wasiApis.wasiClockApi` in scope where it is
      summoned.
  entry .wasiMonotonicClock   a `MonotonicClock` reading `wasi:clocks/monotonic-clock` `now`
      The host's nanosecond counter becomes an `Instant over Monotonic` directly, since that
      timeline ticks in nanoseconds. Needs `wasiApis.wasiClockApi` in scope where it is
      summoned.
